  { number := 54, text := "## 3. Definitions" },
  { number := 55, text := "" },
  { number := 56, text := "### 3.1 Systems Lean" },
  { number := 57, text := "" },
  { number := 58, text := "Lean 4 with linear and affine ownership and Quantitative Type Theory multiplicities restricted to 0, 1, and omega as defined in this clause." },
  { number := 59, text := "" },
  { number := 60, text := "### 3.2 Slake" },
  { number := 61, text := "" },
  { number := 62, text := "The compiler written in Systems Lean that emits freestanding products." },
  { number := 63, text := "" },
  { number := 64, text := "### 3.3 Freestanding product wire" },
  { number := 65, text := "" },
  { number := 66, text := "The binary or C source emitted by Slake that contains no managed Lean runtime and performs no garbage collection." },
  { number := 67, text := "" },
  { number := 68, text := "### 3.4 Multiplicity 0" },
  { number := 69, text := "" },
  { number := 70, text := "Erased at compile time. A multiplicity 0 quantity MUST NOT appear in the freestanding product." },
  { number := 71, text := "" },
  { number := 72, text := "### 3.5 Multiplicity 1" },
  { number := 73, text := "" },
  { number := 74, text := "Linear or affine use: exactly once or at most once." },
  { number := 75, text := "" },
  { number := 76, text := "### 3.6 Multiplicity omega" },
  { number := 77, text := "" },
  { number := 78, text := "Unrestricted use. In this ASCII document the name is the word omega, not a Greek letter." },
  { number := 79, text := "" },
  { number := 80, text := "### 3.7 Residual" },
  { number := 81, text := "" },
  { number := 82, text := "A named, tracked fact that prevents a product from being fully freestanding. Residual honesty means such a fact is explicit. Silence about a remaining managed runtime, garbage collector, or other blocker is nonconforming. Residual honesty does not replace CompCert verified compilation (Clause 4.2 item 6) or a bootstrapped Slake that fully typechecks (Clause 4.2 item 7). Silence is not a `FastC` mark, a `SafeC` mark, or a `FastSafeC` mark." },
  { number := 83, text := "" },
  { number := 84, text := "### 3.8 Requirement keywords" },
  { number := 85, text := "" },
  { number := 86, text := "The key words MUST, MUST NOT, REQUIRED, SHALL, SHALL NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, RECOMMENDED, MAY, and OPTIONAL in this document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119." },
  { number := 87, text := "" },
  { number := 88, text := "### 3.9 CompCert verified compilation" },
  { number := 89, text := "" },
  { number := 90, text := "CompCert verified compilation is compilation by CompCert (`ccomp`) such that the generated assembly is the CompCert verified compilation of that C. `ccomp` is one metric. It is optional. It is not the only metric. It is not a speed claim and it is not comparison language. It is not claimed done. `SafeC` may include CompCert (`ccomp`). When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, that inclusion is optional. `SafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `SafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. `FastSafeC` is the overlap of `SafeC` and `FastC`. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. `FastSafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `FastSafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. An unmarked freestanding C emit is not required to be compiled with `ccomp`. A freestanding C emit marked `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority and is not security priority. Optional does not mean forbidden: a program may still be compiled with `ccomp`. Silence does not select `FastC`, `SafeC`, or `FastSafeC`." },
  { number := 91, text := "" },
  { number := 92, text := "### 3.10 Slake that fully typechecks" },
  { number := 93, text := "" },
  { number := 94, text := "A bootstrapped Slake compiler that typechecks the Systems Lean and Slake sources it is bootstrapped from, including theorems. The typecheck MUST cover all Slake code, including theorems. `FastC` is not an exception. Security priority is not an exception. There is no exception that removes Slake code from that typecheck. Developers and agents MUST always run that typecheck. A user who is building finished code MAY skip running that step. The skip is about who runs the check. It does not exempt the Slake code, and it does not make the bootstrap typecheck done. A `FastC` mark does not make that typecheck done, and it does not waive the typecheck of Slake code. A named subset of modules is not enough. Emitting freestanding C without that typecheck is not enough." },
  { number := 95, text := "" },
  { number := 96, text := "" },
  { number := 97, text := "### 3.11 `SafeMem`" },
  { number := 98, text := "" },
  { number := 99, text := "`SafeMem` means all of the following hold:" },
  { number := 100, text := "" },
  { number := 101, text := "1. There is no use after free and no double free." },
  { number := 102, text := "2. There is no access out of bounds." },
  { number := 103, text := "3. There is no read of owned storage that has not been initialized." },
  { number := 104, text := "4. There is no free while another owner of that block remains in that process." },
  { number := 105, text := "5. There is no loan that outlives its owner or its frame, including an interior loan of a field or a slice on that block." },
  { number := 106, text := "6. There is no `CopyHandle` of an `OwnerHandle` unless an alias protocol allows it (Clause 3.27)." },
  { number := 107, text := "7. There is no write into an `ObjectCow` object or a `PageCow` object while that object is not unique (Clause 3.39 and Clause 3.40)." },
  { number := 108, text := "" },
  { number := 109, text := "`SafeMem` does not imply `PanicFree` (Clause 3.23)." },
  { number := 110, text := "" },
  { number := 111, text := "### 3.12 `SafeThr`" },
  { number := 112, text := "" },
  { number := 113, text := "`SafeThr` means there is no data race on a location that is not atomic, and atomic operations follow the order the program writes. A data race is two threads, one location that is not atomic, at least one write, and no happens before edge." },
  { number := 114, text := "" },
  { number := 115, text := "### 3.13 `SafeLayout`" },
  { number := 116, text := "" },
  { number := 117, text := "`SafeLayout` means layout compatibility (`LayoutCompat`) holds for the program. Where a Fargo gate applies, virtual table compatibility (`VTableCompat`) and binary interface compatibility (`AbiCompat`) are further conjuncts. A claim of LLVM fragment compatibility (`IrFragCompat`) is not claimed proved in this clause (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 118, text := "" },
  { number := 119, text := "### 3.14 `KernelModelOk`" },
  { number := 120, text := "" },
  { number := 121, text := "`KernelModelOk` means the kernel model accepts the program. It is a conjunct of `SafeKernel`. It is not CompCert refinement. It is not a proof of mainline Linux." },
  { number := 122, text := "" },
  { number := 123, text := "### 3.15 `SafeKernel`" },
  { number := 124, text := "" },
  { number := 125, text := "`SafeKernel` of a program means `SafeMem`, `SafeThr`, `SafeLayout`, and `KernelModelOk` all hold for that program. This judgment is not named `SafeLinux`. This document does not claim that mainline Linux is proved." },
  { number := 126, text := "" },
  { number := 127, text := "### 3.16 `CompCertFragment`" },
  { number := 128, text := "" },
  { number := 129, text := "`CompCertFragment` is the fragment of C that excludes a listed set of undefined behavior. It is the fragment CompCert (`ccomp`) compiles when `SafeCompCert` holds. The first fragment is sequential unique C. Official CompCert does not prove threads. The constructor `container_of` is outside this fragment (Clause 3.34). An inline assembly island is outside this fragment. Treating full ISO C as an isomorphism is not a completion condition. Clashes named in Clause 5 and Clause 6.4 are fixtures. A clash that is not yet named is recorded when it is found and is not dropped (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 130, text := "" },
  { number := 131, text := "### 3.17 `CompCertRefines`" },
  { number := 132, text := "" },
  { number := 133, text := "`CompCertRefines` means the generated assembly is the CompCert refinement of that sequential unique C. It ties Clause 3.9 to `CompCertFragment`. It is not claimed done (Clause 6.3). Inhabiting the constructor `holds` is not a claim that a real emit was refined." },
  { number := 134, text := "" },
  { number := 135, text := "### 3.18 `SafeCompCert`" },
  { number := 136, text := "" },
  { number := 137, text := "`SafeCompCert` of a program means `SafeKernel` of that program, `CompCertFragment`, and `CompCertRefines` all hold. `SafeCompCert` implies `SafeKernel`. The converse is False (Clause 4.5 and Clause 5). `SafeKernel` is not by itself `SafeCompCert`. `SafeC` is the family of Safe judgments already named, composed of `SafeMem`, `SafeThr`, `SafeLayout`, `SafeKernel`, and `SafeCompCert`. `SafeC` may include CompCert (`ccomp`). When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, that inclusion is optional. `SafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `SafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. `SafeC` can be combined with `FastC`. That combination is `FastSafeC`. `FastSafeC` is the overlap of `SafeC` and `FastC`. The `FastSafeC` balance rule is that overlap together with the named metrics. The named metrics are the Safe judgments, the `FastC` proofs of parallelism, `atomics`, threading, and proved freedom from locks, the Slake typecheck, and byte identity of emit. CompCert (`ccomp`) is optional and is one metric. It is not the only metric. The overlap is required to prove the best balance between safety and performance. The proof of that balance has not been carried out. That proof is not claimed finished. The rule is not an open question. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. `FastSafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `FastSafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. `ccomp` is one metric of that overlap and is not the only metric. This document does not invent `FastSafeC` block syntax. Further `SafeC` level names besides `FastSafeC` are not specified yet (Clause 6.4). `FastC` is the judgment that a freestanding C emit is performance priority. It is not security priority. `FastC` is not `SafeCompCert`. `FastC` composes with `SafeC`. A program may contain both. `FastC` is proved for the concurrency claims already named for parallelism, `atomics`, threading, and `LockFree` (Clause 3.37, Clause 3.48, and Clause 4.5 item 12). Where `LockFree` holds, those claims are proved free of locks. A Rust `unsafe` block is only an analogy for an explicit mark. This document does not claim Rust semantics. `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is not required to use CompCert (`ccomp`). Optional does not mean forbidden. Block syntax for `FastC` is not specified (Clause 6.4). `FastSafeC` does not replace `SafeC` and does not replace `FastC`." },
  { number := 138, text := "" },
  { number := 139, text := "### 3.19 Reject" },
  { number := 140, text := "" },
  { number := 141, text := "Reject means the `typer` refuses the program before emit. Reject is what a product shows. Reject is not a Fault. A reachable panic is Reject." },
  { number := 142, text := "" },
  { number := 143, text := "### 3.20 Fault" },
  { number := 144, text := "" },
  { number := 145, text := "Fault means an illegal state of Step. Fault belongs to the specification of execution. The emitted binary is not a sanitizer that discovers Fault at run time in place of Reject." },
  { number := 146, text := "" },
  { number := 147, text := "### 3.21 Step" },
  { number := 148, text := "" },
  { number := 149, text := "Step is the operational semantics. Reaching a Fault is a statement about Step. Step is not the emitted ELF." },
  { number := 150, text := "" },
  { number := 151, text := "### 3.22 `TyperSound`" },
  { number := 152, text := "" },
  { number := 153, text := "`TyperSound` is the named implication that if the `typer` accepts a program, execution under Step does not reach a Fault. The `typer` matches Step as Clause 3.21 defines Step. `iris-lean` is not required and is not a dependency. The name is required. The companion fragment implication on `TyperFragment` is proved in the Systems Lean proofs module. The product `typer` is not done. Full product `TyperSound` still lags (Clause 6.4). Systems Lean remains not ready for use." },
  { number := 154, text := "" },
  { number := 155, text := "### 3.23 `PanicFree`" },
  { number := 156, text := "" },
  { number := 157, text := "`PanicFree` of a program means a typed path has no reachable panic, unwrap, expect, failing assertion, typed access out of bounds, overflow that panics, or abort. `PanicFree` does not mean allocation never fails. `PanicFree` does not mean a hardware fault never happens. `SafeMem` does not imply `PanicFree`. `PanicFree` is a separate conjunct from `TyperSound`." },
  { number := 158, text := "" },
  { number := 159, text := "### 3.24 `PanicFreeAOT`" },
  { number := 160, text := "" },
  { number := 161, text := "`PanicFreeAOT` is the named implication that if `PanicFree` holds of a program, execution does not reach a panic. The name is required. The companion fragment implication on `PanicFreeFragment` is proved in the Systems Lean proofs module. The product `AOT` path is not done. Full product `PanicFreeAOT` still lags (Clause 6.4). `PanicFreeAOT` is not `TyperSound`. Systems Lean remains not ready for use." },
  { number := 162, text := "" },
  { number := 163, text := "### 3.25 Bits" },
  { number := 164, text := "" },
  { number := 165, text := "Bits are values with no identity and no `destructor`. `CopyBits` of Bits does not create an owner. A natural number, a Boolean, a numeric machine word, and a structure whose fields are only Bits are Bits. A handle is not Bits. An address word is Bits that name a block, and copying that word creates a second name. An `untyped` address word copy is that second name. It is not `CopyBits` of an `OwnerHandle`. A handle is an address word typed as an `OwnerHandle` or a `LoanHandle`. That typed handle is not copied as Bits." },
  { number := 166, text := "" },
  { number := 167, text := "### 3.26 `UniqueBlock`" },
  { number := 168, text := "" },
  { number := 169, text := "`UniqueBlock` is a judgment of one process identifier and one block. The process identifier is an argument of the judgment. It is not a header field and it is not a reference count. `UniqueBlock` means the owner set of that block in that process has size one. Loans MAY still exist. A loan is not an owner. `UniqueBlock` says nothing about any other process. A `UniqueBlock` has no default reference count header (Clause 4.5). An `OwnerHandle` is a handle typed as an owner. A `LoanHandle` is a handle typed as a loan. Neither handle is Bits (Clause 3.25). A block is `UniqueBlock` in a process only when every page of that object is unique in that process." },
  { number := 170, text := "" },
  { number := 171, text := "Sharing that a crate already records in a header is that crate alias protocol, obtained by correspondence. It is not the default `malloc` path. This document does not invent a ghost owner count as a substitute for that header. An exact owner count on a header type is a later tool and is not claimed here (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 172, text := "" },
  { number := 173, text := "### 3.27 Alias protocol" },
  { number := 174, text := "" },
  { number := 175, text := "An alias protocol is how a heap block whose owner set in a process has size at least two is allowed to die. The constructors named for that protocol are a crate header that already counts references (`Arc` or `Rc` as that crate defines them), `MutexOwns`, and `JoinBeforeFree`. The default protocol is none. The default block is `UniqueBlock` only. `CopyHandle` of an `OwnerHandle` with no alias protocol is Reject, and that clash is False (Clause 4.5 item 3). A reference count field is allowed only as the counted protocol named `Protocol.Counted`. Tracing garbage collection on product clean emit is not a goal (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 176, text := "" },
  { number := 177, text := "### 3.28 Passing modes" },
  { number := 178, text := "" },
  { number := 179, text := "The five passing modes are `CopyBits`, `MoveOwner`, `LoanShared`, `LoanMut`, and `CopyHandle`. No other passing mode is permitted (Clause 4.5)." },
  { number := 180, text := "" },
  { number := 181, text := "1. `CopyBits` copies Bits and does not create an owner." },
  { number := 182, text := "2. `MoveOwner` transfers the owner and the source does not remain an owner." },
  { number := 183, text := "3. `LoanShared` is a shared loan and does not free the block. Shared loans of ranges MAY overlap." },
  { number := 184, text := "4. `LoanMut` is an exclusive loan and does not free the block. A `LoanMut` of a whole object conflicts with any loan of a field of that object. Exclusive loans of ranges are disjoint." },
  { number := 185, text := "5. `CopyHandle` copies an owning handle and creates an extra owner. Without an alias protocol that extra owner is Reject (Clause 3.27)." },
  { number := 186, text := "" },
  { number := 187, text := "Framing is the passing mode on each pointer or file descriptor in a function type: move, loan, or `CopyHandle`. A missing framing mode is the named stash. It is not a sixth passing mode. If the `callee` type does not state one of those modes, the `callee` MAY stash the pointer. `UniqueBlock` does not survive that stash. `UniqueBlock` is false after that stash. Two reads of a unique pointer are not an owner set of size two. A loan of a field is a range loan on the same block, not a new block. A free while any interior loan on that block is live is Reject." },
  { number := 188, text := "" },
  { number := 189, text := "### 3.29 Multiplicities, grades, and omega as an owner" },
  { number := 190, text := "" },
  { number := 191, text := "Systems Lean keeps Quantitative Type Theory multiplicities 0, 1, and omega as defined in Clause 3.4, Clause 3.5, and Clause 3.6. No other multiplicity is permitted (Clause 4.1 item 1). Grade names used with the Safe judgments map onto those multiplicities and onto passing modes as follows." },
  { number := 192, text := "" },
  { number := 193, text := "1. Erased means gone at run time and matches multiplicity 0. An erased quantity does not appear in the freestanding product (Clause 3.4)." },
  { number := 194, text := "2. Once means consumed once, local to the function, and matches a multiplicity 1 use. Grade Once is not lock Once. Grade Once does not imply `UniqueBlock`." },
  { number := 195, text := "3. Trivial means `CopyBits` of Bits only (Clause 3.25 and Clause 3.28). Trivial is the only Safe case of many uses that resemble omega." },
  { number := 196, text := "4. Heap use of omega as an owner is Reject. That Reject is not the Trivial case. The two claims are not the same claim. Language level omega, when a text mentions it, applies only to Bits. It does not apply to a heap `OwnerHandle` (Clause 4.5)." },
  { number := 197, text := "" },
  { number := 198, text := "In this ASCII document the multiplicity name remains the word omega, not a Greek letter (Clause 3.6)." },
  { number := 199, text := "" },
  { number := 200, text := "### 3.30 Profiles" },
  { number := 201, text := "" },
  { number := 202, text := "1. `HostedLinuxLibc` means ISO hosted emit that is product clean (Clause 3.31), with `libc` and Linux in the trusted computing base, and with an entry point named `main`. Sequential unique C on this profile aims at `SafeCompCert`. Threads and `atomics` on this profile are `SafeKernel` and are not by themselves `SafeCompCert`. This profile is additional. It is not the freestanding product wire (Clause 3.3). Clause 4.5 item 6 does not allow that profile to replace the wire. The v28 package names this profile as its default. This document keeps the profile additional. That package does not implement Linux, `glibc`, Wild Child, FixOS, or fit." },
  { number := 203, text := "2. `LibcOnly` means the trusted computing base includes `libc` and the user syntax contains no raw system call." },
  { number := 204, text := "3. `FargoLlvm` means elevation of a Rust crate through LLVM IR, with layout compatibility and Rust trait object tables. The name does not assign fargo a specification number (Clause 3.47)." },
  { number := 205, text := "4. `FreestandingKernel` means ISO freestanding emit that is product clean, with no hosted `libc` in the trusted computing base, and with a kernel or firmware entry. It is not bare metal by itself. It is not the Rust attribute `no_std`." },
  { number := 206, text := "" },
  { number := 207, text := "### 3.31 Words that are not synonyms" },
  { number := 208, text := "" },
  { number := 209, text := "Hosted, ISO freestanding, product clean, bare metal, and the Rust attribute `no_std` are not synonyms." },
  { number := 210, text := "" },
  { number := 211, text := "1. Hosted means the program runs with an operating system and a C library, in the hosted form of C." },
  { number := 212, text := "2. Freestanding means the freestanding form of ISO C. That word is a term of art and this document keeps it. The freestanding product wire (Clause 3.3) is product clean and is distinct from hosted emit. The freestanding form is the section numbered 5.1.2.1 of ISO/IEC 9899:2024. The pin file is absent because the license forbids it (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 213, text := "3. Product clean means the emit has no managed Lean runtime and no managed Idris runtime." },
  { number := 214, text := "4. Bare metal means there is no operating system." },
  { number := 215, text := "5. `no_std` is a Rust attribute. It is not ISO freestanding, it is not product clean, and it is not bare metal." },
  { number := 216, text := "" },
  { number := 217, text := "### 3.32 Correspondence" },
  { number := 218, text := "" },
  { number := 219, text := "Correspondence is a map from judgments on a named fragment to Safe judgments, together with a map back. One map sends a Rust judgment to a Safe judgment or to no judgment. The other map sends a Safe judgment back. Both maps are partial until a gate says otherwise. An isomorphism on a fragment is the further claim that both maps are total on that fragment and compose to identity. Correspondence MUST be in place before any isomorphism claim. Until that theorem exists, text MUST say correspondence of judgments on the fragment and MUST NOT call the maps an isomorphism (Clause 4.5 item 9). Curry-Howard in this clause means a correspondence of rules. It does not mean a finished category isomorphism. A test of the maps is not that theorem." },
  { number := 220, text := "" },
  { number := 221, text := "A language behavior that the source language leaves undefined, leaves to the implementation, or gets silently wrong is Reject, a named Fault, or a named trusted island. Those maps are correspondence. They are not an isomorphism until the identity condition holds. Full ISO C and full ISO C++ as `isomorphisms` are not completion conditions (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 222, text := "" },
  { number := 223, text := "### 3.33 Cons" },
  { number := 224, text := "" },
  { number := 225, text := "Cons of a set of judgments means that set does not prove False from its own constructors and axioms. A clash of two constructors that cannot both hold is False in the companion Lean proofs, not a comment. A clash that would be a vulnerability is Reject or a failed Cons fixture. Cons does not mean every true statement has a proof. It means the claims this document writes do not contradict one another. The fixtures that are False are named in Clause 4.5 item 3 and Clause 5. A fixture that exists only as a test and does not record False does not meet that item." },
  { number := 226, text := "" },
  { number := 227, text := "### 3.34 container_of" },
  { number := 228, text := "" },
  { number := 229, text := "The constructor `container_of` recovers a containing object from an interior field using a documented offset and an identifier of that containing object. It is a `SafeKernel` constructor. Silent recovery, with no documented offset and no identifier, is Reject on a `SafeCompCert` claim. `CompCertFragment` together with `container_of` is False (Clause 4.5 item 3). The containing object is an ordinary data object. The word is not an agent layer. `list_entry` is a `SafeKernel` constructor of the same class, with a parent identifier and a documented offset. Silent recovery on that constructor is Reject on a `SafeCompCert` claim." },
  { number := 230, text := "" },
  { number := 231, text := "### 3.35 Lock catalog" },
  { number := 232, text := "" },
  { number := 233, text := "Phase 0 of the lock catalog is the following names plus a linear `LockToken` (Clause 3.36). Encoding the whole catalog as proved is not required and is not claimed done." },
  { number := 234, text := "" },
  { number := 235, text := "1. `Mutex` is an exclusive lock that owns its payload. Taking the lock yields a `LoanMut` of that payload paired with a `LockToken`. Releasing the lock consumes both and restores the `Mutex`. `Mutex` does not admit shared readers." },
  { number := 236, text := "2. `RwLock` admits many `LoanShared` or one `LoanMut`, and not both at once. A write is permitted only when no reader token and no writer token remain." },
  { number := 237, text := "3. Semaphore is a counter of linear permits. Waiting decrements the counter and returns a permit. Post consumes a permit or creates one when the initial bound allows it. A binary semaphore has maximum one. A counting semaphore has maximum N chosen at the start." },
  { number := 238, text := "4. Permit is the linear token a Semaphore grants. A Permit is not an owner of a heap block." },
  { number := 239, text := "5. `Condvar` is a wait queue attached to a `Mutex`. Waiting consumes a `LockToken` and later returns a new `LockToken`. The type does not by itself solve a missed wake up." },
  { number := 240, text := "6. Barrier collects a fixed number of linear arrival tickets and then releases them. Using one ticket twice is Reject." },
  { number := 241, text := "7. Once initializes a block exactly once. The first writer receives `LoanMut`. Later readers receive `LoanShared`. A second writer is Reject." },
  { number := 242, text := "8. `AffineUnlock` means dropping the guard releases the lock. Forgetting a live `LockToken` is Reject unless a documented leak island applies. This name is not a finished proof." },
  { number := 243, text := "9. A try of the lock (`trylock`) returns a result. It does not panic because the lock is busy." },
  { number := 244, text := "10. A recursive `mutex` is named and is off unless a profile turns it on." },
  { number := 245, text := "11. Lock poisoning is named. This clause does not claim a poisoning proof." },
  { number := 246, text := "12. `SeqLock` is a named lock. The writer receives `LoanMut`. Readers take a snapshot and retry. A proof of `SeqLock` may lag and is not claimed done. `Futex` is an atomic on `ProcessShared` memory, or on a word the kernel owns, and that equation is not a proved theorem. `LockOrder` and `lockdep` come later and are not claimed. A ticket does not prove that deadlock is absent (Clause 3.36 and Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 247, text := "" },
  { number := 248, text := "What a linear `LockToken` changes is that the token cannot be copied, that `Mutex` owns the payload, that `RwLock` is many shared loans or one exclusive loan, and that an `ObjectCow` or `PageCow` write that does not copy when the object is shared is Reject. What it does not provide is absence of deadlock, absence of `livelock`, fairness, or a correct scheduler. What Systems Lean adds, beyond a `mutex` and `atomics` that Rust and C++ already have, is one proposition the kernel model checks and that Fargo can require. That proposition is the linear `LockToken`, the Reject of a copy on write that writes the shared object without copying, the Reject of Secret key material on an `Arc` unless a protocol says those bytes are not secret, and a `UniqueBlock` with no default reference count header." },
  { number := 249, text := "" },
  { number := 250, text := "### 3.36 `LockToken`" },
  { number := 251, text := "" },
  { number := 252, text := "`LockToken` is a linear proof that this thread holds one identified lock. The grade is Once (Clause 3.29). `CopyHandle` of a `LockToken` is Reject. Unlock consumes the `LockToken`. A second unlock is Reject. A missing unlock of a live `LockToken` is Reject. `LockToken` does not prove that deadlock is absent (Clause 4.5)." },
  { number := 253, text := "" },
  { number := 254, text := "### 3.37 `LockFree`" },
  { number := 255, text := "" },
  { number := 256, text := "`LockFree` is the claim that an implementation matches a sequential specification under a named sharing mode, that an operation does not leave an inconsistent state, and that the operation does not block other parties that mode allows. `LockFree` is not the absence of a `Mutex`. `LockFree` is not compare and swap. `LockFree` is not an atomic transaction and it is not the four transaction words in Clause 3.38. The progress claim is that sentence. It is not wait free and it is not obstruction free (Clause 3.55). Proofs of that claim are not claimed done (Clause 6.4). A structure that frees a node and calls itself `LockFree` with no named reclamation protocol is not accepted as `LockFree` (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 257, text := "" },
  { number := 258, text := "### 3.38 ACID words and specification 0003" },
  { number := 259, text := "" },
  { number := 260, text := "Atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability are four words for a transaction. They are not compare and swap. They are not a hardware atomic. They are not a `Mutex`. They are not `LockFree`. Isolation takes a named level. This document does not choose that level for beastdb. Specification `0003_beastdb.md` Clause 3.6 owns proved ACID for beastdb. Clause 3.7 of that specification owns tries that do not use locks. This document does not move those theorems here and does not restate them as theorems of Systems Lean (Clause 4.5). The stack row for beastdb relies on mapped memory and `ZeroView` when those proofs exist. That reliance is not a theorem of this document. The single writer, multiple reader default of beastdb stays in that specification. v28 requires that mode for beastdb, and it allows many writers and many readers on leaves that do not overlap. `AtomicTxn` and durability attach when those proofs exist. Those store rules stay in specification 0003." },
  { number := 261, text := "" },
  { number := 262, text := "### 3.39 `ObjectCow`" },
  { number := 263, text := "" },
  { number := 264, text := "`ObjectCow` is a heap object protocol. A share does not copy bytes. A write when the owner set has size greater than one copies the payload to a new `UniqueBlock`. A write into the shared object while it is not unique is Reject. `ObjectCow` is the protocol that matches a mutating copy of a shared heap object. It is not `PageCow`. It is not `BorrowOrOwned`. It is not `PosixFork` (Clause 3.42). The name `make_mut` denotes `ObjectCow`." },
  { number := 265, text := "" },
  { number := 266, text := "### 3.40 `PageCow`" },
  { number := 267, text := "" },
  { number := 268, text := "`PageCow` is a page table protocol. A shared physical page is protected against writes, and the first store copies that page. The copy is one 4096 byte page. That page is the ordinary 4KB page, and it lines up with a 4KB sector. A larger hardware page is allowed when it improves performance for that one client. That larger hardware page is not a second copy. The copy stays one 4096 byte page (Clause 6.4). `PosixFork` on a private mapping is `PageCow` (Clause 3.42). A write into a `PageCow` page while it is not unique is Reject." },
  { number := 269, text := "" },
  { number := 270, text := "### 3.41 `BorrowOrOwned`" },
  { number := 271, text := "" },
  { number := 272, text := "`BorrowOrOwned` is either a loan or an owned copy. It is the Fargo name for the Rust standard library `enum` that holds one of those two. `BorrowOrOwned` is not `ObjectCow`. `BorrowOrOwned` is not `PageCow`. `PosixFork` is not `BorrowOrOwned`." },
  { number := 273, text := "" },
  { number := 274, text := "### 3.42 `PosixFork`" },
  { number := 275, text := "" },
  { number := 276, text := "`PosixFork` is the POSIX fork operation that creates another process. `ForkJoin` joins threads inside one process. `PosixFork` is not `ForkJoin`. `PosixFork` is not `ObjectCow` and is not `BorrowOrOwned`. On a private mapping, and on anonymous heap pages, `PosixFork` is `PageCow`. File descriptors are duplicated and are not `PageCow`. A mapping that stays shared across the two processes stays shared. A program whose blocks are unique only rejects `PosixFork` (Clause 4.5). `PosixFork` while a Secret with `DontFork` is live is Reject (Clause 3.43 and Clause 3.44). A pin of an address does not survive `PosixFork`. Message passing of an owner uses `MoveOwner`, and message passing of bits uses `CopyBits`. Both travel across threads through a channel. That channel is `ForkJoin` traffic, not `PosixFork` (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 277, text := "" },
  { number := 278, text := "### 3.43 Secret" },
  { number := 279, text := "" },
  { number := 280, text := "Secret means the bytes are not copied by `CopyHandle` or by `CopyBits` into a sink that is not Secret. `ObjectCow` of a Secret is Reject. `PageCow` of a Secret is Reject. Those two clashes are False (Clause 4.5 item 3). `DontFork` is the default while a Secret block is live (Clause 3.44). `WipeOnConsume` applies to Secret (Clause 3.45). A reference counted crate pointer whose payload is key material is Reject unless an explicit protocol says those bytes are not secret. That includes an `Arc` of key material. Keys are an `OwnerHandle` or a pinned `UniqueBlock`. The v28 flagship line also names that pin optional. This document keeps the pin when the key is a `UniqueBlock`, and an `OwnerHandle` key may omit the pin." },
  { number := 281, text := "" },
  { number := 282, text := "### 3.44 `DontFork`" },
  { number := 283, text := "" },
  { number := 284, text := "`DontFork` means `PosixFork` is Reject while this block is live in the forking process." },
  { number := 285, text := "" },
  { number := 286, text := "### 3.45 `WipeOnConsume`" },
  { number := 287, text := "" },
  { number := 288, text := "`WipeOnConsume` means the payload bytes are set to zero before the owner dies. A Secret MUST use `WipeOnConsume` (Clause 4.5 item 11)." },
  { number := 289, text := "" },
  { number := 290, text := "### 3.46 `SafeCt`" },
  { number := 291, text := "" },
  { number := 292, text := "`SafeCt` means control flow and data access do not depend on Secret. `SafeCt` is off by default. A profile that wants `SafeCt` says so. Silence leaves `SafeCt` off and does not turn `SafeCt` on (Clause 4.5). When a profile turns `SafeCt` on, timing, cache behavior, and page faults do not depend on Secret and do not carry Secret (Clause 6.4). Failure to be constant time is not that switch." },
  { number := 293, text := "" },
  { number := 294, text := "### 3.47 Fargo gates A through F" },
  { number := 295, text := "" },
  { number := 296, text := "Fargo is the `SurmountSystems` cargo fork named fargo. This document does not assign fargo a specification number (Clause 6.2 item 2 and Clause 6.4). Gates A through F are obligations a correspondence MUST prove (Clause 3.32). They are not a specification number and they are not a numbered file." },
  { number := 297, text := "" },
  { number := 298, text := "1. Gate A. The syntax fragment contains no `unsafe` code, or `unsafe` code is isolated behind an `OwnerHandle` interface the Safe judgments can see." },
  { number := 299, text := "2. Gate B. Every type from the Rust compiler (`rustc`) maps to Bits, an `OwnerHandle`, a `LoanHandle`, or Reject." },
  { number := 300, text := "3. Gate C. Each function passing mode maps to one of the five passing modes in Clause 3.28." },
  { number := 301, text := "4. Gate D. Layout compatibility holds for every type that crosses a binary interface or is stored in a structure Fargo emits." },
  { number := 302, text := "5. Gate E. Virtual table compatibility holds for every trait object Fargo emits." },
  { number := 303, text := "6. Gate F. Product clean emit, as C or as LLVM IR, contains no managed Lean runtime and no managed Idris runtime." },
  { number := 304, text := "" },
  { number := 305, text := "A crate that meets Gate A and does not meet Gate D, Gate E, and Gate F is not Safe (Clause 4.5 item 9). This clause does not make Fargo run cargo. This document states what a gate MUST prove. Elevation of these gates in the fargo fork is not claimed implemented (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 306, text := "" },
  { number := 307, text := "### 3.48 Emit split" },
  { number := 308, text := "" },
  { number := 309, text := "The emit split is which compiler runs. CompCert (`ccomp`) applies when `SafeCompCert` holds, on sequential unique C. LLVM or `cc` applies when only `SafeKernel` holds, including `atomics`, device registers, explicit packed vector operations, and assembly islands. The LLVM or `cc` path is not proved by CompCert. CompCert does not prove layout compatibility against `rustc`. That layout obligation is the LLVM and Fargo path. The primary emission target of Slake stays freestanding, runtimeless C (Clause 4.2 item 2). `HostedLinuxLibc` is an additional profile (Clause 3.30). It is not a silent replacement of that primary target. CompCert (`ccomp`) is one metric. It is optional. It is not the only metric. When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of CompCert (`ccomp`) is optional. `SafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `SafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. `FastSafeC` is the overlap of `SafeC` and `FastC`. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. `FastSafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `FastSafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. `ccomp` is one metric of that overlap and is not the only metric. The other metrics are the Safe judgments, the `FastC` proofs of parallelism, `atomics`, threading, and proved freedom from locks, the Slake typecheck, and byte identity of emit. The `FastSafeC` balance rule is that overlap together with those named metrics. The overlap is still required to prove the best balance between safety and performance. The proof of that balance has not been carried out. That proof is not claimed finished. The rule is not an open question. An unmarked freestanding C emit is not required to be compiled with `ccomp`. Security priority does not always require `ccomp`. Optional does not mean forbidden: a program may still be compiled with `ccomp`. `ccomp` being an optional metric does not make CompCert verified compilation done. A freestanding C emit marked `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority and is not security priority. Silence does not select `FastC`, `SafeC`, or `FastSafeC`. `FastC` points at the parallelism, `atomics`, threading, and `LockFree` clauses already named in this document. It does not state a new memory model." },
  { number := 310, text := "" },
  { number := 311, text := "### 3.49 `LinkFace`" },
  { number := 312, text := "" },
  { number := 313, text := "`LinkFace` is the exported ownership of a typed object or archive: symbol names, layouts, and the passing modes `MoveOwner`, `LoanShared`, `LoanMut`, `CopyHandle`, and Bits. A classic linker concatenates sections and resolves symbols. It does not check that a relocation is a loan. Until Wild Child exists, `cc`, `ld`, `lld`, and the upstream Wild linker stay in the trusted computing base and do not preserve `UniqueBlock` (Clause 6.4). A symbol clash, a layout mismatch, or a `UniqueBlock` exported as Bits is Reject. That last clash is False (Clause 4.5 item 3). The typed link output is one ELF file or one shared object whose combined program is `SafeKernel`, or `SafeCompCert` when every linked unit is `CompCertFragment`. A typed library may stay Safe for typed callers. The process that calls `untyped` code is not Safe." },
  { number := 314, text := "" },
  { number := 315, text := "### 3.50 `ThreadShared` and `ProcessShared`" },
  { number := 316, text := "" },
  { number := 317, text := "`ThreadShared` means a block is reachable from two threads in one process. It is not a map. `ProcessShared` means two virtual mappings name one physical page under a shared map. `ThreadShared` is not `ProcessShared`. Treating those two words as one word is False (Clause 4.5 item 16)." },
  { number := 318, text := "" },
  { number := 319, text := "### 3.51 `AtomicCell` and compare and swap" },
  { number := 320, text := "" },
  { number := 321, text := "`AtomicCell` is a location that MUST be touched only with atomic operations. `HwAtomic` is a hardware atomic. Compare and swap is a technique. The technique includes compare and exchange, weak compare and exchange, load linked with store conditional, and a double width compare and swap when the hardware has it. It is not `AtomicCell`. It is not `LockFree`. It is not an atomic transaction. Compare and swap on a plain `UniqueBlock` pointer is Reject. Compare and swap on a cell that is not `AtomicCell` is Reject. Those two clashes are False (Clause 4.5 item 3). `AtomicCell` and `AtomicTxn` remain unfinished product theories (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 322, text := "" },
  { number := 323, text := "### 3.52 Correspondence maps" },
  { number := 324, text := "" },
  { number := 325, text := "Correspondence maps are statements, not an isomorphism theorem. The named maps are `Corresponds`, `toSafeC`, and `toRust`. One direction sends a Rust judgment to a Safe judgment or to no judgment. The other sends a Safe judgment back. Both maps stay partial until a gate says they are total on a named fragment and compose to identity (Clause 3.32). This document MUST NOT name a theorem `Isomorphism`. Naming a theorem `Isomorphism` is False (Clause 4.5 item 3)." },
  { number := 326, text := "" },
  { number := 327, text := "### 3.53 `UbClass`" },
  { number := 328, text := "" },
  { number := 329, text := "`UbClass` is a named class of language behavior that the source language leaves undefined, leaves to the implementation, or gets silently wrong. Correspondence for each named class is a map from that class to an optional Fault and a map from a source construct to an optional class. An isomorphism on a fragment is allowed only when those maps are total on that fragment and compose to identity. Listing some classes is not that theorem." },
  { number := 330, text := "" },
  { number := 331, text := "Named C classes include signed integer overflow, an uninitialized read of automatic storage, use after free, double free, pointer arithmetic or an array subscript out of bounds, a data race, strict aliasing, a `restrict` violation, an invalid free, an `unsequenced` side effect, a shift exponent out of range, following a null pointer, pointer provenance misuse, a variable length array or a GNU statement expression or an empty GNU structure as Reject on `CompCertFragment`, overlapping `memcpy` ranges, and use of the old pointer after `realloc`. Named C++ classes include iterator invalidation after mutation, a reference bound to an expired temporary, `this` after lifetime ends, signed overflow, placement new lifetime, a `destructor` during unwind, a data race on a shared pointer control block, and mutation of a `const` object through `const_cast`. Named Rust classes include `unsafe` that manufactures aliasing exclusive loans, uninitialized storage without `MaybeUninit`, integer overflow as a named operation that `PanicFree` cares about, and `forget` of a live lock guard. Each named class in this clause is Reject before emit or is Fault under Step. That includes effective type beside strict aliasing, a pointer one past the end, C++ narrowing, and a data race on a shared pointer control block unless that block is used atomically. A `destructor` during unwind does not apply when that behavior is specified. Fargo does not elevate raw C++. A class that CompCert does not model stays `SafeKernel` only, and the island is named. An invalid free includes a pointer that did not come from `malloc` and a wrong size story. The Rust integer overflow split is a debug panic versus wrapping. A C++ fragment gets a correspondence row only when a crate or an emit path needs that fragment. A class that CompCert already excludes is Reject before `ccomp`. Calling C Safe while that C has signed overflow is a Cons clash. The product Fault table is not claimed complete (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 332, text := "" },
  { number := 333, text := "### 3.54 `ZeroView` and `Pinned`" },
  { number := 334, text := "" },
  { number := 335, text := "`ZeroView` means the layout of a type in memory is exactly the buffer bytes. Access is a shared loan of that view. `CopyBits` of the payload is Reject. `Unmap` while the view is live is Reject. `ValidView` adds alignment, bounds, an optional endian (`Le`, `Be`, or `Native`), a schema or generation tag, and an optional checksum. A relative pointer inside an archive is `RelPtr`. An implementation of the view is not claimed done (Clause 6.4). `Pinned` means the address MUST NOT move. `Pinned` is not immortal. A pin of an address does not survive `PosixFork`. Treating a pin as surviving `PosixFork` is False (Clause 4.5 item 3). The address stays pinned while a `ZeroView` lives. Unpinning a pinned address is documented. The reasons include a value that points at itself, `DMA`, device registers, `mlock`, and `ZeroView`." },
  { number := 336, text := "" },
  { number := 337, text := "### 3.55 Wait free and obstruction free" },
  { number := 338, text := "" },
  { number := 339, text := "Wait free means an operation finishes in a bounded number of its own steps. Obstruction free means an operation finishes if it runs alone. Those two claims are not `LockFree`. The three progress claims are these definitions together with Clause 3.37. Proofs of them are not claimed done (Clause 6.4)." },
  { number := 340, text := "" },
  { number := 341, text := "### 3.56 `UsesLock` and `LockHandled`" },
  { number := 342, text := "" },
  { number := 343, text := "`UsesLock` means a path waits for exclusion. `LockHandled` means every such path releases a ticket or a guard and has a documented try, timeout, or spin policy. Those identifiers are named. Treating them as present theories is False (Clause 6.4 items 42 and 46). Clients should not observe a leaked or unbounded exclusion." },
  { number := 344, text := "" },
  { number := 345, text := "### 3.57 Reject fixtures" },
  { number := 346, text := "" },
  { number := 347, text := "Reject fixtures are product facing. The `typer` MUST refuse each of the following before emit (Clause 3.19). A comment is not enough." },
  { number := 348, text := "" },
  { number := 349, text := "1. `CopyHandle` of an `OwnerHandle`, a `LockToken`, a Permit, or a Secret." },
  { number := 350, text := "2. Trivial grade on a Handle." },
  { number := 351, text := "3. Erased used at runtime." },
  { number := 352, text := "4. Once used twice in one body." },
  { number := 353, text := "5. Free while the owner set in that process has size at least two." },
  { number := 354, text := "6. Free while an interior loan is live." },
  { number := 355, text := "7. `ObjectCow` write in place while the owner set has size greater than one." },
  { number := 356, text := "8. `PageCow` write in place while the mapper set has size greater than one." },
  { number := 357, text := "9. `PosixFork` in a program whose blocks are unique only." },
  { number := 358, text := "10. `PosixFork` while a Secret with `DontFork` is live." },
  { number := 359, text := "11. `MoveOwner` of pinned device registers." },
  { number := 360, text := "12. A data race on a `ThreadShared` location that is not atomic." },
  { number := 361, text := "13. A reference count header on `UniqueBlock` without a shared owner protocol." },
  { number := 362, text := "14. Load with Release or store with Acquire." },
  { number := 363, text := "15. Compare and swap on a cell that is not `AtomicCell`." },
  { number := 364, text := "16. `Unmap` or close while a loan or a `ZeroView` is live." },
  { number := 365, text := "17. `UniqueBlock` exported on a `LinkFace` as Bits." },
  { number := 366, text := "18. Stash of a loan into a global." },
  { number := 367, text := "19. Forget of a live `LockToken` unless a documented leak island applies." },
  { number := 368, text := "20. A reachable unwrap or typed access out of bounds on a `PanicFree` claim." },
  { number := 369, text := "21. Unlock of a `Mutex` without a `LockToken`." },
  { number := 370, text := "22. A second `RwLock` write while a read token lives." },
  { number := 371, text := "23. `CopyHandle` of a key typed `OwnerHandle` in a cryptography fixture." },
  { number := 372, text := "" },
  { number := 373, text := "Pass fixtures that a later product MAY accept are named in Clause 6.4. They are not claimed green in this specifications repository." },
  { number := 374, text := "" },
  { number := 375, text := "## 4. Core requirements" },
  { number := 376, text := "" },
  { number := 377, text := "### 4.1 Language core" },
  { number := 378, text := "" },
  { number := 379, text := "1. Systems Lean MUST support only the three Quantitative Type Theory multiplicities 0, 1, and omega defined in Clause 3. No other multiplicity is permitted." },
  { number := 380, text := "2. Multiplicity 1 MUST enforce linear or affine resource discipline (exactly once or at most once)." },
  { number := 381, text := "3. Any definition intended for the freestanding product wire MUST NOT require managed heap allocation that needs a runtime, MUST NOT require garbage collection, and MUST NOT require residual Lean runtime services." },
  { number := 382, text := "" },
  { number := 383, text := "### 4.2 Compiler product (Slake)" },
  { number := 384, text := "" },
  { number := 385, text := "1. The compiler named in this specification MUST be written in Systems Lean." },
  { number := 386, text := "2. The primary emission target of Slake MUST be freestanding, runtimeless C." },
  { number := 387, text := "3. A secondary LLVM IR emission path MAY be provided later for use with the Rust ecosystem. That path is optional and is not required for conformance." },
  { number := 388, text := "4. The freestanding product wire MUST contain no managed Lean runtime and MUST NOT perform garbage collection." },
  { number := 389, text := "5. The compiler MUST maintain residual honesty: any residual that prevents a fully freestanding product MUST be explicitly named and tracked." },
  { number := 390, text := "6. CompCert (`ccomp`) is one metric. It is optional. It is not the only metric. `SafeC` may include CompCert (`ccomp`). When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, that inclusion is optional. `SafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `SafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. `SafeC` can be combined with `FastC`. That combination is `FastSafeC`. `FastSafeC` is the overlap of `SafeC` and `FastC`. The `FastSafeC` balance rule is that overlap together with the named metrics. The named metrics are the Safe judgments, the `FastC` proofs of parallelism, `atomics`, threading, and proved freedom from locks, the Slake typecheck, and byte identity of emit. CompCert (`ccomp`) is optional and is one metric of that rule. It is not the only metric. The overlap is required to prove the best balance between safety and performance. The proof of that balance has not been carried out. That proof is not claimed finished. The rule is not an open question. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. `FastSafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `FastSafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. An unmarked freestanding C emit is not required to be compiled with `ccomp`. Security priority does not always require `ccomp`. Optional does not mean forbidden: a program may still be compiled with `ccomp`. `ccomp` being an optional metric does not make CompCert verified compilation done. Naming a residual does not make that compilation done, and it does not make `ccomp` the only metric. Silence does not select `FastC`, `SafeC`, or `FastSafeC`. A freestanding C emit marked `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority. `FastC` is not security priority. A `FastC` mark does not make CompCert verified compilation done, does not make a bootstrapped Slake that fully typechecks done, and does not make byte identity of emit true. A `FastSafeC` mark does not waive byte identity of emit. This document does not claim CompCert is pinned under this specifications repository's `ref/`. The Form 1 pin lives in the Systems Lean product tree at `ref/CompCert`. The reference side of delta reconciliation is the major compilers. CompCert `ccomp`, `CompCertTSO`, gcc, LLVM, and `cc` are in that set, and being a reference target does not mean that compiler satisfies Clause 4.2 item 6. `CompCertTSO`, gcc, LLVM, and `cc` do not satisfy Clause 4.2 item 6 merely by being reference targets." },
  { number := 391, text := "7. Bootstrapping a Slake compiler that fully typechecks (Clause 3.10) is a REQUIRED core validation step. A conforming Slake MUST typecheck the Systems Lean and Slake sources it is bootstrapped from, including theorems. The typecheck MUST cover all Slake code, including theorems. `FastC` is not an exception. Security priority is not an exception. There is no exception that removes Slake code from that typecheck. Developers and agents MUST always run that typecheck. A user who is building finished code MAY skip running that step. The skip is about who runs the check. It does not exempt the Slake code, and it does not make the bootstrap typecheck done. A `FastC` mark does not make that typecheck done, and it does not waive the typecheck of Slake code. Naming a residual MUST NOT substitute for this step. A claim that the freestanding product hosts itself from generated C is a distinct claim and does not satisfy this step by itself." },
  { number := 392, text := "" },
  { number := 393, text := "### 4.3 Repository and hygiene rules" },
  { number := 394, text := "" },
  { number := 395, text := "These rules apply to novel Systems Lean and Slake source trees (product repositories). They are distinct from the citation pins of this specifications repository under `0000_spec.md` Clause 12. This specifications repository's `ref/` holds external specifications this repository cites as 0000 Clause 12 pins. The Systems Lean product tree's `ref/` is where Lean 4, Idris 2, CompCert, Rust, and similar upstream language and compiler trees live as Form 1 git submodules. This document does not claim that Lean 4, Idris 2, CompCert, or Rust are pinned under this specifications repository's `ref/`." },
  { number := 396, text := "" },
  { number := 397, text := "1. Novel Systems Lean and Slake source MUST be released under the Unlicense." },
  { number := 398, text := "2. Upstream reference trees MUST live under a clearly separated `ref/` directory of that product tree, MUST use these directory names, and MUST retain their original licenses:" },
  { number := 399, text := "\t1. `ref/Idris2` for Idris 2 (publisher git https://github.com/idris-lang/Idris2.git)." },
  { number := 400, text := "\t2. `ref/lean4` for Lean 4 (publisher git https://github.com/leanprover/lean4.git)." },
  { number := 401, text := "\t3. `ref/CompCert` for CompCert (publisher git https://github.com/AbsInt/CompCert.git). CompCert is not Unlicense." },
  { number := 402, text := "\t4. `ref/rust` for Rust (publisher git https://github.com/rust-lang/rust.git)." },
  { number := 403, text := "\tAgent skill packs MUST NOT be placed under that product tree's `ref/`; they live under `skills/` when present." },
  { number := 404, text := "3. Novel work MUST be expressed in at most three languages: Idris 2, Lean 4 (including Systems Lean), and pure Nix flakes." },
  { number := 405, text := "4. Shell scripts that float free of Nix MUST NOT be used for novel product paths. Pure Nix is REQUIRED for build and packaging of novel components." },
  { number := 406, text := "" },
  { number := 407, text := "### 4.4 Relationship to the Surmount stack" },
  { number := 408, text := "" },
  { number := 409, text := "1. Systems Lean is the REQUIRED implementation language for the core of beastdb (specified in `0003_beastdb.md`), the Lean Machine formalization layer, components of the Smart Symbolic Supercompiler specified in `0004_SSSSS.md` that are safety critical, and future high assurance rewrites under the FixOS vision. Application and neurosymbolic integration for SSSSS live in `0004_SSSSS.md`. Lean Machine is specified in `0013_Lean_Machine.md`." },
  { number := 410, text := "2. All such components MUST be able to target the freestanding Slake path." },
  { number := 411, text := "3. Recursive Self Improvement is specified in `0006_RSI.md`. That document is practice for Surmount specifications and agents. It is not a compiler requirement of this document." },
  { number := 412, text := "" },
  { number := 413, text := "" },
  { number := 414, text := "### 4.5 Safe judgments" },
  { number := 415, text := "" },
  { number := 416, text := "1. `SafeCompCert` of a program MUST imply `SafeKernel` of that program (Clause 3.18)." },
  { number := 417, text := "2. The converse MUST be False. `SafeKernel` of a program MUST NOT be treated as `SafeCompCert` of that program. The judgment MUST NOT be named `SafeLinux`. This document MUST NOT claim that mainline Linux is proved (Clause 3.15)." },
  { number := 418, text := "3. Each named Cons fixture MUST be False in the companion Lean proofs and MUST be Reject in a product that claims the Safe judgments. The fixtures are `UniqueBlock` together with `CopyHandle` of that owner and no alias protocol, Secret together with `ObjectCow`, Secret together with `PageCow`, `CompCertFragment` together with `container_of`, `PanicFree` together with a reachable unwrap, a unique only program together with `PosixFork`, `CompCertFragment` together with an inline assembly island, `LockFree` together with a free of a node that names no reclamation protocol, `SafeMem` together with a data race on a cell that is not atomic, a `LinkFace` that exports `UniqueBlock` as Bits, language level omega as an owner on a heap `OwnerHandle`, a pin that survives `PosixFork`, a theorem named `Isomorphism`, `glibc` `malloc` treated as `UniqueBlock` against `untyped` C, a file that lacks a `Mutex` treated as `LockFree`, and the page cache treated as `RCU`. A comment is not enough. A test that does not record the clash as False is not enough. A proof hole written as `sorry` MUST NOT be counted as success. The `UniqueBlock` plus `CopyHandle` fixture MUST NOT be encoded a second time as a different mash (Clause 3.33)." },
  { number := 419, text := "4. On any path that claims a Safe judgment, CompCert (`ccomp`) MUST be applied only when `SafeCompCert` holds, and that application MUST start with sequential unique C (Clause 3.16 and Clause 3.48). Clause 4.2 item 6 stays in force: CompCert (`ccomp`) is one metric. It is optional. It is not the only metric. An unmarked freestanding C emit is not required to be compiled with `ccomp`. Naming a residual does not make CompCert verified compilation done, and it does not make `ccomp` the only metric. When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of `ccomp` is optional. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. `FastSafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `FastSafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. A freestanding C emit marked `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority and is not security priority. Silence does not select `FastC`, `SafeC`, or `FastSafeC`. `FastC` is not `SafeCompCert`. `SafeKernel` is not by itself `SafeCompCert`." },
  { number := 420, text := "5. When only `SafeKernel` holds, LLVM or `cc` MAY be used. That path MUST NOT be called proved by CompCert (Clause 3.48). Clause 4.2 item 3 remains optional and is not required for conformance. The memory models MUST be the Linux kernel memory model, the C memory model, and the C++ memory model, together with one LLVM IR that Rust and Linux kernel C both accept for memory layout and for virtual tables. Systems Lean MUST be a first class language for Rust and for Linux kernel C on that IR. The IR version MUST be the one those two accept. This document MUST NOT invent a version number. When the memory layout of updated system code and of programs in flight stays consistent, a restart MUST NOT be required. This item MUST NOT be read as a claim that a running kernel was verified." },
  { number := 421, text := "6. The primary emission target of Slake MUST remain freestanding, runtimeless C (Clause 4.2 item 2). `HostedLinuxLibc` MUST be an additional profile and MUST NOT replace that primary target or the freestanding product wire (Clause 3.3, Clause 3.30, and Clause 3.31)." },
  { number := 422, text := "7. Systems Lean MUST keep only the multiplicities 0, 1, and omega (Clause 4.1 item 1 and Clause 3.29). Heap use of omega as an owner MUST be Reject. Many uses of Bits that are Trivial, meaning `CopyBits`, MUST NOT be described as that Reject. The two claims MUST NOT be collapsed. `UniqueBlock` MUST be indexed by a process identifier and a block. That identifier MUST NOT be a reference count. A `UniqueBlock` MUST NOT carry a default reference count header. Sharing MUST be the alias protocol of a crate header that already counts references, not a ghost owner count invented by this document (Clause 3.26 and Clause 3.27)." },
  { number := 423, text := "8. Every passing mode MUST be one of `CopyBits`, `MoveOwner`, `LoanShared`, `LoanMut`, or `CopyHandle` (Clause 3.28). No sixth passing mode is permitted." },
  { number := 424, text := "9. Fargo gates A through F MUST be read as obligations on a correspondence (Clause 3.47). They MUST NOT be read as a specification number. fargo MUST NOT be given a specification number by this document (Clause 6.2 item 2). A crate that has completed only Gate A MUST NOT be called Safe. If a note names a crate gate, it MUST name the highest completed gate. A proof hole written as `sorry` on Gate D, Gate E, or Gate F MUST NOT be described as success. Correspondence MUST be stated before any isomorphism claim. Until both maps are total on that fragment and compose to identity, text MUST say correspondence of judgments on the fragment and MUST NOT call the maps an isomorphism (Clause 3.32)." },
  { number := 425, text := "10. The names `TyperSound` and `PanicFreeAOT` MUST exist (Clause 3.22 and Clause 3.24). The `typer` MUST match Step as Clause 3.21 defines Step. `iris-lean` MUST NOT be required and MUST NOT be a dependency. Companion fragment implications MAY be proved while the product `typer` and the product `AOT` path still lag (Clause 6.4). A lagging product proof MUST NOT be presented as done. A proof hole written as `sorry` MUST NOT be counted as success." },
  { number := 426, text := "11. On a private mapping, and on anonymous heap pages, `PosixFork` MUST be `PageCow` and MUST NOT be `ObjectCow` or `BorrowOrOwned` (Clause 3.42). A program whose blocks are unique only MUST Reject `PosixFork`. `DontFork` MUST be the default for a live Secret, and `PosixFork` while that Secret is live MUST be Reject (Clause 3.43 and Clause 3.44). `SafeCt` MUST be off unless a profile explicitly turns it on (Clause 3.46). When a profile turns `SafeCt` on, timing, cache behavior, and page faults MUST NOT carry Secret. The `PageCow` copy MUST stay one 4096 byte page. A larger hardware page MAY be used when it improves performance for that one client and MUST NOT be a second copy. A Secret MUST use `WipeOnConsume` before the owner dies (Clause 3.45). A pin of an address MUST NOT be treated as surviving `PosixFork` (Clause 3.54)." },
  { number := 427, text := "12. `LockToken` MUST be linear (Clause 3.36). `CopyHandle` of a `LockToken` MUST be Reject. A second unlock of one `LockToken` MUST be Reject. A missing unlock of a live `LockToken` MUST be Reject. `LockToken` MUST NOT be read as a proof that deadlock is absent. `LockFree` MUST NOT be defined as the absence of a `Mutex`, MUST NOT be defined as compare and swap, and MUST NOT be defined as `atomicity`, consistency, isolation, and durability (Clause 3.37 and Clause 3.38). Those four words MUST NOT be defined as compare and swap. A file that lacks a `Mutex` MUST NOT be called `LockFree`. Proved ACID for beastdb and tries that do not use locks MUST remain in `0003_beastdb.md` and MUST NOT be restated as theorems of this document." },
  { number := 428, text := "13. The Reject fixtures in Clause 3.57 MUST be Reject before emit. Silence about a listed fixture MUST NOT be treated as Pass." },
  { number := 429, text := "14. `UbClass` names in Clause 3.53 MUST be correspondence of a class to an optional Fault and of a source construct to an optional class. Those maps MUST NOT be called an isomorphism until they are total on a named fragment and compose to identity (Clause 3.32)." },
  { number := 430, text := "15. Correspondence maps `Corresponds`, `toSafeC`, and `toRust` MUST exist as statements (Clause 3.52). They MUST NOT be a theorem named `Isomorphism`." },
  { number := 431, text := "16. `ThreadShared` MUST NOT be `ProcessShared` (Clause 3.50). `AtomicCell` MUST NOT be compare and swap (Clause 3.51). `glibc` `malloc` MUST NOT be claimed as `UniqueBlock` against `untyped` C. The page cache MUST NOT be `RCU`." },
  { number := 432, text := "" },
  { number := 433, text := "" },
  { number := 434, text := "## 5. Conformance" },
  { number := 435, text := "" },
  { number := 436, text := "A Systems Lean implementation or Slake product conforms to this specification if and only if all of the following hold:" },
  { number := 437, text := "" },
  { number := 438, text := "1. It restricts Quantitative Type Theory multiplicities to 0, 1, and omega as defined in Clause 3." },
  { number := 439, text := "2. It emits freestanding products free of managed Lean runtime and garbage collection, or it names and tracks every residual that prevents a fully freestanding product (Clause 4.2 item 5). Residual honesty in this item applies to the freestanding product wire. It does not excuse a missing CompCert validation step on an unmarked freestanding C emit, and it does not excuse a missing Slake bootstrap typecheck. When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of `ccomp` is optional, so a missing `ccomp` on that `SafeC` emit is not the unmarked obligation. A `FastC` mark is not residual silence. A `FastC` mark does not waive the typecheck of Slake code. Security priority does not waive that typecheck." },
  { number := 440, text := "3. It places novel source under the Unlicense and keeps upstream trees under that product tree's `ref/` with the directory names in Clause 4.3 and with original licenses (Clause 4.3 items 1 and 2)." },
  { number := 441, text := "4. It uses only the three permitted languages for novel work (Clause 4.3 item 3) and does not use shell scripts that float free of Nix for novel product paths (Clause 4.3 item 4)." },
  { number := 442, text := "5. Named high assurance components that claim conformance to Clause 4.4 can target the freestanding Slake path." },
  { number := 443, text := "6. CompCert (`ccomp`) is one metric (Clause 4.2 item 6). It is optional. It is not the only metric. An unmarked freestanding C emit is not required to be compiled with `ccomp`. When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of `ccomp` is optional. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. A freestanding C emit marked `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority and is not security priority. That mark does not make CompCert verified compilation done. A `FastSafeC` mark does not waive byte identity of emit." },
  { number := 444, text := "7. A bootstrapped Slake compiler fully typechecks the Systems Lean and Slake sources it is bootstrapped from, including theorems (Clause 4.2 item 7). The typecheck covers all Slake code, including theorems. `FastC` is not an exception. Security priority is not an exception. There is no exception that removes Slake code from that typecheck. Developers and agents always run that typecheck. A user who is building finished code MAY skip running that step. The skip is about who runs the check. It does not exempt the Slake code, and it does not make the bootstrap typecheck done. A `FastC` mark does not make that typecheck done, and it does not waive the typecheck of Slake code." },
  { number := 445, text := "8. Where it claims `SafeCompCert` for a program, it also meets `SafeKernel` for that program (Clause 4.5 item 1)." },
  { number := 446, text := "9. It does not treat `SafeKernel` as sufficient for `SafeCompCert`. That converse is False (Clause 4.5 item 2)." },
  { number := 447, text := "10. It rejects the named Cons clashes, and those clashes are False, including `UniqueBlock` together with `CopyHandle` of that owner and no alias protocol, Secret together with `ObjectCow`, Secret together with `PageCow`, `CompCertFragment` together with `container_of`, `PanicFree` together with a reachable unwrap, a unique only program together with `PosixFork`, `CompCertFragment` together with an inline assembly island, `LockFree` together with a free of a node that names no reclamation protocol, `SafeMem` together with a data race on a cell that is not atomic, a `LinkFace` that exports `UniqueBlock` as Bits, language level omega as an owner on a heap `OwnerHandle`, a pin that survives `PosixFork`, a theorem named `Isomorphism`, `glibc` `malloc` treated as `UniqueBlock` against `untyped` C, a file that lacks a `Mutex` treated as `LockFree`, and the page cache treated as `RCU` (Clause 4.5 item 3)." },
  { number := 448, text := "11. On any path that claims a Safe judgment, it applies CompCert (`ccomp`) only when `SafeCompCert` holds, starting with sequential unique C (Clause 4.5 item 4). CompCert (`ccomp`) is one metric. It is optional. It is not the only metric. An unmarked freestanding C emit is not required to be compiled with `ccomp`. Silence does not select `FastC`, `SafeC`, or `FastSafeC`. When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of `ccomp` is optional. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. A marked `FastC` emit that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority and is not security priority." },
  { number := 449, text := "12. Where it uses LLVM or `cc` because only `SafeKernel` holds, it does not call that path proved by CompCert (Clause 4.5 item 5). It keeps the Linux kernel memory model, the C memory model, and the C++ memory model, and one LLVM IR that Rust and Linux kernel C both accept for memory layout and virtual tables. When the memory layout of updated system code and of programs in flight stays consistent, it does not require a restart. It does not claim that a running kernel was verified." },
  { number := 450, text := "13. It keeps freestanding, runtimeless C as the primary Slake emit, and it does not replace that emit or the freestanding product wire with `HostedLinuxLibc` (Clause 4.5 item 6). `HostedLinuxLibc` MAY exist only as an additional profile." },
  { number := 451, text := "14. It keeps multiplicities 0, 1, and omega, it rejects heap use of omega as an owner, it does not describe Trivial `CopyBits` of Bits as that rejection, and a `UniqueBlock` has no default reference count header (Clause 4.5 item 7). Clause 5 item 1 is not weakened by this item." },
  { number := 452, text := "15. Every passing mode it accepts is one of the five named in Clause 3.28 (Clause 4.5 item 8)." },
  { number := 453, text := "16. It does not treat Fargo gates A through F as a specification number, it does not call a Gate A only crate Safe, and it does not call judgment maps an isomorphism before both maps are total on the named fragment and compose to identity (Clause 4.5 item 9)." },
  { number := 454, text := "17. The companion encoding names `TyperSound` and `PanicFreeAOT` (Clause 4.5 item 10). Companion fragment implications on `TyperFragment` and `PanicFreeFragment` are proved. The `typer` matches Step as Clause 3.21 defines Step. `iris-lean` is not required and is not a dependency. This item does not require the product `typer` or the product `AOT` path to be finished." },
  { number := 455, text := "18. It treats `PosixFork` on a private mapping as `PageCow` and not as `ObjectCow`, it rejects `PosixFork` for a program whose blocks are unique only, it defaults a live Secret to `DontFork`, it wipes a Secret before the owner dies, and it leaves `SafeCt` off unless a profile turns `SafeCt` on. When a profile turns `SafeCt` on, timing, cache behavior, and page faults do not carry Secret. The `PageCow` copy stays one 4096 byte page. A larger hardware page is allowed when it improves performance for that one client and is not a second copy (Clause 4.5 item 11)." },
  { number := 456, text := "19. It treats `LockToken` as linear, it does not define `LockFree` as the absence of a `Mutex` or as compare and swap, it does not call a file that lacks a `Mutex` `LockFree`, it does not define `atomicity`, consistency, isolation, and durability as compare and swap, and it leaves proved ACID for beastdb and tries that do not use locks in `0003_beastdb.md` (Clause 4.5 item 12)." },
  { number := 457, text := "20. It rejects the Clause 3.57 fixtures before emit (Clause 4.5 item 13)." },
  { number := 458, text := "21. It treats `UbClass` maps as correspondence statements and does not call those maps an isomorphism before the identity condition holds (Clause 4.5 item 14)." },
  { number := 459, text := "22. It states correspondence maps `Corresponds`, `toSafeC`, and `toRust` and does not name a theorem `Isomorphism` (Clause 4.5 item 15)." },
  { number := 460, text := "23. It keeps `ThreadShared` distinct from `ProcessShared`, it keeps `AtomicCell` distinct from compare and swap, it does not claim `glibc` `malloc` as `UniqueBlock` against `untyped` C, and it does not treat the page cache as `RCU` (Clause 4.5 item 16)." },
  { number := 461, text := "" },
  { number := 462, text := "Optional LLVM IR emission (Clause 4.2 item 3) is not required for conformance. Where that path, or `cc`, is used because only `SafeKernel` holds, it is not proved by CompCert (Clause 5 item 12)." },
  { number := 463, text := "" },
  { number := 464, text := "## 6. Residual" },
  { number := 465, text := "" },
  { number := 466, text := "This section is normative for residual honesty of this document. Silence about an unpublished specification, an unassigned number, an unimplemented requirement, or an out of scope item named in this document is nonconforming for this specification as a document. A parenthetical planned file name in Scope is not the residual record." },
  { number := 467, text := "" },
  { number := 468, text := "### 6.1 Product tree and language pins" },
  { number := 469, text := "" },
  { number := 470, text := "1. Systems Lean and Slake work is ongoing and is not ready for use. This document MUST NOT be read as claiming that Systems Lean or Slake never started or do not exist." },
  { number := 471, text := "2. The Systems Lean product tree that already holds Lean 4, CompCert, Idris 2, and Rust as git submodules is `/home/hunter/Projects/ai/iso`. This specifications repository MUST NOT copy those four trees into this repository's `ref/`. Existing pins of this repository remain. New pins are added when the cited artifact is present." },
  { number := 472, text := "3. Recursive Self Improvement is specified in `0006_RSI.md`. That document is practice for Surmount specifications and agents. It is not a compiler requirement of this document." },
  { number := 473, text := "" },
  { number := 474, text := "### 6.2 Unassigned names" },
  { number := 475, text := "" },
  { number := 476, text := "1. Lean Machine is specified in `0013_Lean_Machine.md`. That document is specification 0013. Application and neurosymbolic integration live in `0004_SSSSS.md`." },
  { number := 477, text := "2. fargo, Carbonado, and SSSP have no assigned specification numbers. This document does not create a numbered file for fargo, for Carbonado, or for SSSP." },
  { number := 478, text := "" },
  { number := 479, text := "### 6.3 Product requirements that are not ready for use" },
  { number := 480, text := "" },
  { number := 481, text := "CompCert (`ccomp`) is one metric. It is optional. It is not the only metric. It is not claimed done. A bootstrapped Slake compiler that fully typechecks is a required validation step. It is not claimed done. When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of CompCert (`ccomp`) is optional. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. `FastSafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `FastSafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. A freestanding C emit marked `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority and is not security priority. Silence does not select `FastC`, `SafeC`, or `FastSafeC`. That `FastC` mark does not make CompCert verified compilation done, does not make the Slake typecheck done, and does not make byte identity of emit true. A `FastSafeC` mark does not waive byte identity of emit. The `FastSafeC` balance rule is the overlap of `SafeC` and `FastC` together with the named metrics in Clause 4.2 item 6. The proof of that balance has not been carried out. That proof is not claimed finished. The rule is not an open question. The typecheck MUST cover all Slake code, including theorems. `FastC` is not an exception. Security priority is not an exception. There is no exception that removes Slake code from that typecheck. Developers and agents MUST always run that typecheck. A user who is building finished code MAY skip running that step. The skip is about who runs the check. It does not exempt the Slake code, and it does not make the bootstrap typecheck done. A `FastC` mark does not waive the typecheck of Slake code. Sharper tests for finished code, user, developer, and agent are not specified yet. Byte identity of emit is required for a conforming product and is not claimed true. Byte identity of emit remains required for a conforming product and is not claimed true. The match is pursued by delta reconciliation in `0004_SSSSS.md`: the same input goes to the Lean symbolic rules and to the reference compiler, both outputs are decompiled, and the deltas are labeled. A `FastC` mark does not waive the match. A user who may skip running the Slake typecheck is not excused from the match. The live work is not claimed finished. A conformance fixture that sets those steps true is not that live claim. Further `SafeC` level names besides `FastSafeC` are not specified yet. `FastC` block syntax is not specified. This document does not invent `FastSafeC` block syntax. The reference side of delta reconciliation is the major compilers. CompCert `ccomp`, `CompCertTSO`, gcc, LLVM, and `cc` are in that set. A reference target does not satisfy Clause 4.2 item 6. `CompCertTSO`, gcc, LLVM, and `cc` do not satisfy that item. The unpublished 1.0.0 wording that treated CompCert verified compilation as a required core validation step is withdrawn. Clause 3.9 and Clause 4.2 item 6 are the current rule. The work is not claimed done." },
  { number := 482, text := "" },
  { number := 483, text := "" },
  { number := 484, text := "### 6.4 Safe judgments that are not claimed done" },
  { number := 485, text := "" },
  { number := 486, text := "This subsection records Safe judgment work that this document names and does not claim is done. It does not retire Clause 6.1, Clause 6.2, or Clause 6.3. Systems Lean and Slake remain ongoing and are not ready for use. The Safe judgments are not a claim that a `SafeC` package is ready for use. This document MUST NOT be read as claiming that Systems Lean or Slake never started or do not exist (Clause 6.1 item 1)." },
  { number := 487, text := "" },
  { number := 488, text := "1. Encodings of these claims belong in `lean/SystemsLeanSpec.lean` and proofs belong in the Systems Lean proofs module under `lean/`. A mash of those modules is nonconforming (Clause 1.2 item 3). This prose is not those proofs. Companion proofs that the Clause 5 fixtures are False are not claimed done by this prose. A theorem closed with `sorry` MUST NOT be counted as success. Systems Lean and Slake remain not ready for use." },
  { number := 489, text := "2. CompCert verified compilation of an unmarked freestanding C emit is optional, is not the only metric, and is not claimed done (Clause 6.3). When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of CompCert (`ccomp`) is optional. For `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. `FastSafeC` can include `ccomp`, and `FastSafeC` is not required to include `ccomp`. `ccomp` is one metric of that overlap and is not the only metric. The `FastSafeC` balance rule is that overlap together with the named metrics in Clause 4.2 item 6. The overlap is required to prove the best balance between safety and performance. The proof of that balance has not been carried out. That proof is not claimed finished. The rule is not an open question. The `SafeCompCert` path does not make CompCert verified compilation done and does not retire the bootstrapped Slake typecheck. The typecheck MUST cover all Slake code, including theorems. `FastC` is not an exception. Security priority is not an exception. There is no exception that removes Slake code from that typecheck. Developers and agents MUST always run that typecheck. A user who is building finished code MAY skip running that step. The skip is about who runs the check. It does not exempt the Slake code, and it does not make the bootstrap typecheck done. A marked `FastC` performance priority emit that is not combined with `SafeC` is not security priority. An unmarked freestanding emit is not required to include `ccomp`. Byte identity of emit remains REQUIRED for a conforming product and is not claimed true (Clause 6.3). A `FastC` mark does not waive that match. A `FastSafeC` mark does not waive that match. Further `SafeC` level names besides `FastSafeC` are not specified yet. `FastC` block syntax is not specified. This document does not invent `FastSafeC` block syntax." },
  { number := 490, text := "3. This document uses freestanding as the ISO C term of art. A `ref/` pin freezes an external text. That text is treated as the specification, and proofs are built from it so the program can be reproduced in a proven, high assurance way. The emit standard is freestanding ISO C, which is the conformance class in ISO/IEC 9899:2024 (edition 5, publication date 2024-10-31, ISO catalog 82075). ISO and IEC do not publish that text in git. The official PDF may not be stored in this public repository. The publisher page is [ISO/IEC 9899:2024](https://www.iso.org/standard/82075.html) (accessed: 2026-10-13). The pin file is absent because the license forbids it. The report is `/home/hunter/.agents/reports/goal-iso-c-pin.md`." },
  { number := 491, text := "4. fargo has no assigned specification number (Clause 6.2 item 2). Gates A through F do not assign fargo a number. This document does not create a numbered file for fargo and does not create a numbered file for `SafeC`." },
  { number := 492, text := "5. The v28 `SafeC` lake package is leftover in the Systems Lean product tree. It is not a shipped product in this specifications repository. This document does not create a numbered `SafeC` file. This document does not create a `SafeC` directory here. The Operator assigned number 0015 to the session bus, and the numbered file is not in this tree because the draft is missing. Phase 0 of that lake package, including types, glossary constructors, Reject fixtures, and four profile documents, is not claimed done." },
  { number := 493, text := "6. The v28 `SafeC` package directory layout, including the lake file, the `toolchain` pin, the package README, `TCB`.md, the package changelog, and the inner Lean modules named in v28 section 14, is not a tree in this specifications repository. This document does not create those paths. The v28 package changelog records the standing rules and the closed meaning forks. That file is not in this repository. The v28 package names a Lean module `InconsistencyReport` that lists the Cons clashes and fails closed. That module is not this repository checker `inconsistency_report`, and it is not created here." },
  { number := 494, text := "7. This document does not edit the Systems Lean product tree at `/home/hunter/Projects/ai/iso`. Implementation of the v28 `SafeC` package there stays out of scope until the operator expands scope. This specifications repository still MUST NOT copy Lean 4, CompCert, Idris 2, or Rust into this repository's `ref/` (Clause 6.1 item 2)." },
  { number := 495, text := "8. Companion fragment proofs of the `TyperSound` implication on `TyperFragment` and of the `PanicFreeAOT` implication on `PanicFreeFragment` are proved in the Systems Lean proofs module. The names stay required (Clause 3.22, Clause 3.24, and Clause 4.5 item 10). The product `typer` and the product `AOT` path still lag. The requirement is specified: the `typer` matches Step as Clause 3.21 defines Step, and `iris-lean` is not required and is not a dependency. Full product implications are not claimed done. If a later product proof does not hold, this item remains the record that the product proofs lag. A proof hole MUST NOT be counted as success. Systems Lean remains not ready for use." },
  { number := 496, text := "9. The v28 toolbox has no cap of two constructs. A missing model means the model is not written yet, not that the language is smaller by silence. Regions, an arena, capabilities such as a file descriptor and a `LockToken`, dependent lengths, `MaybeUninit`, Send and Sync, `PanicFree`, a CompCert subset, and optional CHERI or `MTE` hardware tags are not finished models in this document." },
  { number := 497, text := "10. The v28 first `allocator` is `malloc` and free as `UniqueBlock` in the calling process, with no reference count field. Typed callers MAY treat a `glibc` return as that `UniqueBlock`. An `untyped` second pointer is outside Safe. This document does not claim that `glibc` `malloc` is proved." },
  { number := 498, text := "11. An exact owner count on a header type is not the default `malloc` path. That default path is `malloc` and free as `UniqueBlock` with no reference count field (item 10)." },
  { number := 499, text := "12. On a private mapping, and on anonymous heap pages, `PosixFork` is `PageCow` (Clause 3.42 and Clause 4.5 item 11). A mapping that stays shared across the two processes stays shared (Clause 3.42). `ProcessShared` means two virtual mappings name one physical page under a shared map (Clause 3.50). File backed maps, `memfd`, and two mappings of one object in one process are named. A larger hardware page is allowed when it improves performance for that one client, and it is not a second copy (Clause 3.40). Device and memory mapped I/O, `DMA` and an `IOMMU`, `sendfile` and splice, `userfaultfd`, and `io_uring` with registered buffers are named map modes and are not claimed proved. A block reachable from two threads in one process is not the same word as two virtual mappings of one physical page." },
  { number := 500, text := "13. Wild Child, the v28 planned Systems Lean typed linker, is not implemented in this repository. Until it exists, cc, `ld`, `lld`, and the upstream Wild linker stay in the trusted computing base and do not preserve `UniqueBlock`. Incremental linking is a later Wild goal and is not a v28 `SafeC` phase. This document does not implement a linker. v28 records that the published Wild Child repository is still a Rust fork of Wild and is not the Systems Lean typed linker. Wild Child exists so that a link keeps the proof. Incremental link is useful and is not a phase of this document." },
  { number := 501, text := "14. Explicit v28 packed vector operations (the v28 SIMD topic) require a documented alignment. They are not automatic packed vector operations and they are not an atomic location. A full opcode catalog is not claimed done. `AVX2` is 256 bit integer and floating point packed vector operations on `ymm` registers, including gathers, permutes, and variable shifts. The documented alignment for `AVX2` aligned loads and stores is 32 bytes. That figure states the alignment this item requires for those aligned operations. It does not replace the rule that every explicit packed vector operation requires a documented alignment. Unaligned forms are separate instructions and do not satisfy the aligned form. The `CPUID` check for `AVX2` is leaf 7, `subleaf` 0, `EBX` bit 5. The gcc and LLVM target feature token is `avx2`. `FMA` (`FMA3`) is fused multiply add with one rounding step. The exact product plus the addend is rounded once. Proofs use that single rounding as the reference semantics. An `AVX2` multiply followed by an add is not the same bits as that single rounding, and it is not the reference. The `CPUID` check for `FMA` is leaf 1, `ECX` bit 12. The gcc and LLVM target feature token is `fma`. `BMI2` is scalar bit manipulation. The instructions are `PDEP`, `PEXT`, `BZHI`, `MULX`, `SARX`, `SHLX`, `SHRX`, and `RORX`. `BMI2` is scalar, not packed. It is not a packed vector operation, so it is not the SIMD topic of this item or of item 66. It is inside the same target feature set. The `CPUID` check for `BMI2` is leaf 7, `subleaf` 0, `EBX` bit 8. The gcc and LLVM target feature token is `bmi2`. For each of these features, the target triple names `x86-64`, and that name does not by itself enable the feature. The Linux `x86_64` fragment in item 22 remains the layout and System V convention name. It is not a claim that the triple enables `AVX2`, `FMA`, or `BMI2`. The target attribute or the function attribute MUST list the token explicitly (`+avx2`, `+fma`, or `+bmi2`) on that target or on that function. These features MUST NOT be enabled by a blanket `-march=native`, and they MUST NOT be enabled by a global `-mavx2`, `-mfma`, and `-mbmi2` that covers every translation unit. This item does not invent opcodes beyond the instructions named in this item." },
  { number := 502, text := "15. A v28 `ZeroView` has a layout in memory that is exactly the buffer bytes. `ValidView` adds alignment, bounds, an optional endian (`Le`, `Be`, or `Native`), a schema or generation tag, and an optional checksum. `Access` is a `LoanShared` of a `ZeroView` and is not `CopyBits` of the payload. A relative pointer inside an archive is `RelPtr`. `Unmap` while that view is live is Reject. An implementation of the view is not claimed done." },
  { number := 503, text := "16. v28 `CompCertTSO` is research and is not the default compiler. Official CompCert does not prove threads. This document does not claim that a `CompCertTSO` validation step is done. This item does not make `CompCertTSO` into Clause 4.2 item 6." },
  { number := 504, text := "17. The memory models are the Linux kernel memory model, the C memory model, and the C++ memory model, together with one LLVM IR that Rust accepts and that Linux kernel C accepts for memory layout and for virtual tables. Systems Lean is a first class language for Rust and for Linux kernel C on that IR. The IR version is the one those two accept for memory layout and virtual tables. This document does not invent a version number. `LKMM` names the Linux kernel memory model. A running kernel is not claimed verified. Ordinary Systems Lean follows the shape of Rust. Linux and C tricks are explicit `SafeKernel` constructors, including `container_of`, `list_entry`, `pthread_mutex_unlock`, the barriers of the Linux kernel memory model, and copy on write of one 4096 byte page for `PosixFork`." },
  { number := 505, text := "18. v28 `allocators` after `malloc`, namely an arena, a bump `allocator`, a slab, a buddy `allocator`, a page `allocator`, and kernel allocation, share one interface: allocation introduces a `UniqueBlock` and free consumes it. This document does not freeze one `allocator` theory. Those later `allocator` names are not claimed implemented. When an arena dies it frees every block it owns. A free of one child block is Reject." },
  { number := 506, text := "19. v28 Fargo elevation of gates A through F is not implemented in the fargo fork. Proofs of gates D through F MAY lag. Unsafe code, unions, and Drop glue that Fargo cannot encode stay outside the encoded fragment for that crate. Aeneas, Charon, and `Hax` are the preferred extractors for safe fragments, and this specifications repository does not implement those extractors. A proof hole is not a record that a gate succeeded. fargo stays unnumbered (Clause 6.2 item 2)." },
  { number := 507, text := "20. `SeqLock` is a named lock and its proof may lag (Clause 3.35). `Futex` is an atomic on `ProcessShared` memory, or on a word the kernel owns, and that equation is not a proved theorem. `Futex` remains inside the `HostedLinuxLibc` trusted computing base until a kernel `workstream` implements it, and that `workstream` is not claimed done. `LockOrder` and `lockdep` come later and are not claimed. A ticket does not prove that deadlock is absent. The phase 0 lock names plus a linear `LockToken` are not implemented in this specifications repository. Those lock failure modes are Reject before emit, rather than a runtime `lockdep` check. A proof hole is not success." },
  { number := 508, text := "21. The `LockFree` progress claim is the Clause 3.37 sentence. It is not wait free and it is not obstruction free (Clause 3.55). Those proofs are not claimed done. `RwLock` fairness is not claimed. `LockFree` is not the absence of `Mutex`." },
  { number := 509, text := "22. Layout compatibility, virtual table compatibility, binary interface compatibility, and LLVM fragment compatibility are requirements on the Linux x86_64 fragment, beyond the `SafeLayout` sentence. The data layout string is the `rustc` string for the `toolchain` Rust and Linux kernel C both accept for memory layout and virtual tables. This document does not invent an LLVM version number. Structure padding uses C representation first. A Rust representation is a second fragment only when tests exist. The pin file named Layout.lean is package layout and is not created in this specifications repository. A partial proof is not success, and these requirements stay named. The same requirement records byte order, pointer width, and the ABI alignment of `i1`, `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64`, `i128`, `float`, and `double`. `LayoutCompat` is stated for each fixture type. `VTableCompat` is stated for one trait object fixture. Each fixture records whether it uses a fat pointer or a virtual pointer at offset zero. `AbiCompat` covers functions whose arguments are only scalars and C representation structures, on the Linux x86_64 System V convention as `rustc` implements it for that fragment. `IrFragCompat` covers `alloca`, `load`, `store`, and `getelementptr` on those types, and `call` and `ret` of functions that meet `AbiCompat`. The whole LLVM language reference is not a completion condition. `undef`, `poison`, `invoke`, `landingpad`, the full vector instruction set, and `addrspacecast` are outside that completion condition." },
  { number := 510, text := "23. The v28 `atomics` catalog is the Rust surface `AtomicBool`, `AtomicU8` through `AtomicU64`, `AtomicUsize`, the signed twins, and `AtomicPtr`, plus load, store, swap, compare and exchange, weak compare and exchange, and integer fetch operations. A load with Release ordering and a store with Acquire ordering are Reject. Consume ordering is not in the first order set. Sequentially consistent order is first. Weaker orders come later and are not claimed proved. `TSX` and hardware transactional memory are named and are not an atomic transaction and are not beastdb ACID. Compare and swap stays a technique and is not `LockFree`. The catalog beyond that Rust surface also names `fence`, `compiler_fence`, `AtomicU128` when the hardware has it, a double width compare and swap, `cmpxchg16b` when the hardware has it, load linked with store conditional as an implementation of compare and swap, and kernel bitmap `atomics`. Phase 3 proves sequentially consistent order before any weaker order." },
  { number := 511, text := "24. The durability calls are write, `pwrite`, `fsync`, `fdatasync`, `msync`, and `O_DIRECT`. That call list is not `atomicity`, consistency, isolation, and durability. Specification 0003 remains the owner of durability for beastdb. A write ahead log attached to beastdb is not a theorem of this document." },
  { number := 512, text := "25. v28 `LibcSL` and v28 `KernelSL` are rewrite `workstreams` and are not claimed done. This document does not invent a public product repository for either name. The first `LibcSL` surface names `memcpy`, `memset`, `malloc`, and free, in that order, and that surface is not done work here. The first `KernelSL` slice is startup, exit, and a documented system call slice. `PageCow` for POSIX fork is not in that first slice. A `futex` as an atomic on process shared memory, or on a word the kernel owns, is not a proved theorem. `strdup` allocates a `UniqueBlock` and then uses `CopyBits` of payload bytes. A `FILE` is a `Mutex` around a buffer, or it is unique when the program has one thread. A page `allocator` is a `UniqueBlock` or an arena. Firmware on `FreestandingKernel` has no hidden heap. `allocator` values there are explicit arenas, or there is no `allocator`. Memory mapped input and output is `Pinned` and immortal. Interrupt handling is not in that first slice. The page cache is counted pages, mappings, and a dirty or locked state. Read copy update is a later lookup tool and is not that cache. `Untyped` `glibc` allocation is not `UniqueBlock`." },
  { number := 513, text := "26. v28 phased delivery of a `SafeC` package, including phases 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, C, P, F, L, K, W, and B, is not claimed done in this repository. A trusted base note per profile is not a file in this tree. The package done condition that its README say `SafeC` v26 is not the version line of specification 0002. The prompt file itself is v28. A lake build of that package is not a build of this specifications repository. Phase 0 names types, glossary constructors, Reject fixtures, and four profile documents. Phase 1 names unique `HostedLinuxLibc` emit. Phase 2 names CompCert on sequential unique C, and that phase is faithfulness only. Phase 3 names `ForkJoin`, `Mutex`, `RwLock`, `AffineUnlock`, sequentially consistent `atomics`, and semaphore types. A condition variable proof may lag, and a proof hole is not success. Phase 4 names explicit packed vector operations and not automatic packed vector operations. Phase 5 names layout, virtual table, binary interface, and the LLVM fragment. Later phase letters name `ObjectCow` fixtures, `PageCow` types, Fargo gates, the first `LibcSL` calls, `KernelSL` startup, Wild Child as a separate `workstream`, and beastdb attachment of `LockFree` and the four transaction words as not a done condition of this package. A proof hole is not success." },
  { number := 514, text := "27. Classic v28 undefined behavior and language quirks for C, for C++, and for Rust are named as `UbClass` in Clause 3.53. Each named class is Reject before emit or is Fault under Step. The product Fault table is not complete. Those maps stay correspondence until an isomorphism gate holds. Full ISO C and full ISO C++ as `isomorphisms` are not completion conditions. A class that CompCert already excludes is Reject before `ccomp`. A class that CompCert does not model stays `SafeKernel` only, and the island is named. An open emit or behavior delta is recorded and is not dropped." },
  { number := 515, text := "28. Reproducing an existing artifact by writing rules is not a specification number. The v28 name `sleanify` has no specification number, is not a shipped skill pack, and names the operator loop that ingests an implementation, writes rules, runs Cons, reconciles deltas, and emits a `SafeC` or Slake rewrite. Lean Machine remains specification 0013 and is not a `SafeC` module. This document is not the Surmount product `roadmap`. FixOS remains the vision in Clause 4.4 and is not claimed shipped. The product name fit, and a file system built on beastdb, have no specification numbers and this document does not invent public repositories for them. `grok-oss` is not `SafeC`. The `gabriel` repository is out of `SafeC` scope. `bao-tree` is not FixOS and is not fit. The north star that names `sleanify`, `LibcSL`, `KernelSL`, Fargo, Wild Child, and beastdb is not a phase 0 done condition and is not a completion theorem." },
  { number := 516, text := "29. Other v28 typing tools, including regions, capabilities, and dependent lengths, are named and are not substitutes already proved in this document. The `typer` matches Step as Clause 3.21 defines Step. `iris-lean` is not required and is not a dependency. Hardware tagging is not a replacement for `UniqueBlock`." },
  { number := 517, text := "30. `ForkJoin` joins threads inside one process and is not `PosixFork` (Clause 3.42). Message passing of an owner uses `MoveOwner`. Message passing of bits uses `CopyBits`. Both travel across threads through a channel. That channel is not a second process. An implementation of the channel is not claimed done." },
  { number := 518, text := "31. When a profile turns `SafeCt` on, timing, cache behavior, and page faults do not carry Secret (Clause 3.46). `SafeCt` stays off by default. This document does not claim that any profile turns `SafeCt` on. Meeting that aim is not claimed." },
  { number := 519, text := "32. `pthread_mutex_unlock` is a `SafeKernel` constructor. Unlock without holding the lock is Reject. That constructor is not claimed proved. CompCert does not prove that constructor." },
  { number := 520, text := "33. Reclamation protocols are hazard pointers, an epoch, a quiescent state, and read copy update. A `LockFree` claim that frees a node with no named reclamation protocol is not accepted as `LockFree`. That clash is a Clause 5 Cons fixture proved False in the Systems Lean proofs module. That proof does not make Systems Lean or Slake ready for use. Those protocols are not claimed implemented." },
  { number := 521, text := "34. These five constructor clashes are Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module: `CompCertFragment` together with an inline assembly island, a link face that exports `UniqueBlock` as bits, language level omega applied to a heap `OwnerHandle`, a unique only profile together with `PosixFork`, and `SafeMem` together with a data race on a cell that is not atomic. Systems Lean and Slake remain not ready for use." },
  { number := 522, text := "35. A pin means the address must not move, and a pin does not survive `PosixFork` (Clause 3.54). An immortal device register is `Pinned` and is not a pin of an ordinary object. An arena introduces a `UniqueBlock`, and ending the arena consumes that block (item 18). A file descriptor is a capability and is not Bits. Uninitialized owned storage is named, and a read of those bytes before initialization is Reject. Bare metal is later and is not the `FreestandingKernel` profile this document requires now. Tracing garbage collection on product clean emit is not a goal. When the memory layout of updated system code and of programs in flight stays consistent, a restart is not required. This document does not claim that a running kernel was verified." },
  { number := 523, text := "36. The `PageCow` copy is one 4096 byte page (Clause 3.40). A larger hardware page is allowed when it improves performance for that one client. That larger hardware page is not a second copy." },
  { number := 524, text := "37. A canonical public GitHub organization for the systems-lean or beastdb repositories is out of scope for this one client system. That is a decision. Holy Lean, as named in specification 0013, is not a site vocabulary catalog, and this document does not adopt that v28 sentence. The public site at surmount.systems is not a clause of specification 0002." },
  { number := 525, text := "38. Other type tools that prove no use after free or no undefined behavior, including tools named in v28 section 21, are not already proved substitutes for the Safe judgments in this document." },
  { number := 526, text := "39. The closed meaning forks in v28 section 19 are decisions. `SafeCompCert` implies `SafeKernel` and the converse is false. Freestanding is the ISO term of art and product clean is separate. The first `allocator` is `malloc` as `UniqueBlock` with no header. The `typer` Rejects before emit and Step uses Fault. Interior pointers are field loans, and `container_of` is `SafeKernel`. `ObjectCow` and `PageCow` both exist. `ProcessShared` is a physical page, a virtual block, and the map catalog. Phase 0 of the lock catalog is a linear `LockToken` together with `AffineUnlock`. Framing is `MoveOwner`, a loan, and `CopyHandle`. Linking is Wild Child, and `untyped` code is a trusted computing base island. This document does not invent a public product repository for a name that does not have one. A canonical public GitHub organization stays out of scope (item 37)." },
  { number := 527, text := "40. A trusted computing base note per profile (`TCB`.md in a `SafeC` package) is not a file in this specifications repository." },
  { number := 528, text := "41. This document MUST NOT name a theorem `Perfect` or `ProvablyPerfect`. Those identifiers are banned as theorem names. A completion theorem is not that ban. Naming a theorem `Perfect` or `ProvablyPerfect` is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Systems Lean remains ongoing and is not ready for use." },
  { number := 529, text := "42. `AtomicCell` is a location touched only with atomic operations (Clause 3.51). `AtomicTxn` names a transaction that uses the four words in Clause 3.38. `AtomicTxn` is not compare and swap, not `LockFree`, and not `AtomicCell`. Neither identifier is a present theory, and an atomic cell library is not ready for use. Treating `AtomicCell`, `AtomicTxn`, `UsesLock`, and `LockHandled` as present theories is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module." },
  { number := 530, text := "43. `Rust atomic types map to AtomicCell` plus methods, and that map is the catalog in item 23. The map is correspondence and is not a finished isomorphism." },
  { number := 531, text := "44. Compare and swap on a plain `UniqueBlock` pointer is Reject (Clause 3.51). Treating that compare and swap as accepted is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Systems Lean and Slake remain not ready for use." },
  { number := 532, text := "45. Compare and swap on a cell that is not `AtomicCell` is Reject (Clause 3.51). Treating that compare and swap as accepted is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. A data race on a cell that is not atomic is a different clash. Systems Lean and Slake remain not ready for use." },
  { number := 533, text := "46. The identifiers `UsesLock` and `LockHandled` are named here. Treating those identifiers as present theories is the same Cons False fixture as `AtomicCell` and `AtomicTxn` in item 42. `LockFree` MUST NOT mean that a `Mutex` is absent." },
  { number := 534, text := "47. The `default trait object fat pointer fields` are size, align, drop, and methods, and that layout is residual correspondence, not an isomorphism. Clause 3.30 item 3 names Rust trait object tables and does not freeze those four fields as proved." },
  { number := 535, text := "48. The `memcpy equation` is `CopyBits` of Bits, or a `LoanShared` source plus a `LoanMut` destination of the same length. Naming `memcpy` in the first `LibcSL` list is not that equation. Treating the `memcpy equation` as a present theorem is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module." },
  { number := 536, text := "49. `LinkFace` exports symbol names, layouts, and the passing modes `MoveOwner`, `LoanShared`, `LoanMut`, `CopyHandle`, and Bits (Clause 3.49). Cons applies to every such export. A typed link input is a Safe object or archive that carries that face. Those proofs are not claimed done. Treating a typed link input as proved is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Treating Cons on every export as proved is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. A link face that exports `UniqueBlock` as bits is a different clash, and that clash is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Those names do not make Systems Lean or Slake ready for use." },
  { number := 537, text := "50. beastdb `Props` are named here. Cons on those `Props` applies only when they attach. Cons on those `Props` when they do not attach is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Specification 0003 remains the owner of beastdb ACID. beastdb remains not ready for use." },
  { number := 538, text := "51. A `shift exponent out of range` is Reject before emit or is Fault under Step (Clause 3.53). Classic undefined behavior as a blanket residual is not that class." },
  { number := 539, text := "52. The `sleanify observation list` is layout, aliasing, whether behavior is undefined, and side channel surface, and that list is not a shipped skill pack. Naming `sleanify` as an operator loop is not that list." },
  { number := 540, text := "53. Slake is the compiler written in Systems Lean (Clause 3.2). This specifications repository does not give Slake a second git tree." },
  { number := 541, text := "54. Linear and affine use in Clause 3.5 and Clause 4.1 item 2 MUST NOT be read as a proof of all undefined behavior. Those types stay correspondence. They are not an isomorphism." },
  { number := 542, text := "" },
  { number := 543, text := "55. This document MUST name loans for framing, locks, file descriptors, and use after free as the four way use after free list. Loans for that coverage are `MoveOwner`, `LoanShared`, `LoanMut`, and `CopyHandle` (Clause 3.28). That coverage is not claimed implemented. A sentence that residual means no model yet MUST mean the model is not written yet, not that the language is smaller by silence." },
  { number := 544, text := "56. A named Cons clash fixture MUST stay a `Bool` encoding set to false. That fixture is not a claim that every clash is listed. Treating the whole ISO C language as a finished completion condition is not allowed. The north star sentence that names `sleanify`, `LibcSL`, `KernelSL`, Fargo, Wild Child, and beastdb MUST NOT be read as a phase 0 done condition." },
  { number := 545, text := "57. Linux kernel memory model barriers are the barriers of that model (item 17). Copy on write of one 4096 byte page for POSIX fork is `PageCow` (Clause 3.40). The `typer` matches Step as Clause 3.21 defines Step. An exact owner count is later work and is not the default `malloc` path." },
  { number := 546, text := "58. The identifiers `PhysPage` and `VirtBlock` are named here and are not present theories. The map modes are `MAP_SHARED`, `MAP_PRIVATE`, and `MAP_ANON`, plus file backed maps, `shm`, and `memfd` (item 12). `msync`, `mprotect`, `munmap`, `madvise`, `mremap`, and `mlock` are named map operations. `munmap` or close while a loan or a `ZeroView` is live is Reject. `ThreadShared` is not `ProcessShared`. Those two words MUST stay distinct. Treating them as the same word is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module." },
  { number := 547, text := "59. Until Wild Child exists, `ld`, `lld`, and the upstream Wild linker stay in the trusted computing base. This document MUST classify what can lock. An `ObjectCow` or `PageCow` write that does not copy when the object is shared is Reject (Clause 3.35)." },
  { number := 548, text := "60. Wait free proofs and obstruction free proofs are not claimed done. The four ACID words stay in specification 0003. Shrinking the trusted computing base is a later goal and is not claimed done. That shrink replaces a symbol with a proved module of the same ABI." },
  { number := 549, text := "61. `Block` names a region with identity. The kinds are a heap allocation, the address of a stack local, an immortal global, and a mapped range. `UniqueBlock` is the owner judgment on that region in one process (Clause 3.26). `Map` names the map catalog in item 58. `Site` names where a block lives, and the cases are stack, heap, immortal, and mapped. Erased is not a `Site`. `Immortal` means that storage is never freed, and immortal is not omega used as an owner. A device register is one immortal case, and it is `Pinned` (Clause 3.54). `MMIO` is memory mapped input and output, and it is `Pinned` and immortal (item 25). Loads and stores of those registers are volatile. `Arena` introduces a `UniqueBlock` (item 18). When an arena dies it frees every block it owns, and a free of one child block is Reject. `Fd` is a file descriptor capability and is not Bits (item 35). Closing that descriptor consumes the owner. A duplicate is a second owner. Those identifiers are named here and are not present theories. `Pinned` means the address MUST NOT move. `Pinned` is not immortal. A pin of an address does not survive `PosixFork` (Clause 3.54). A `Site` projects list is not a clause of this specifications repository." },
  { number := 550, text := "62. The identifiers `Grade`, `Once`, omega as an owner, and `Frame` are named here. Bare metal later MUST NOT replace the `FreestandingKernel` profile this document requires now. The crate shapes `no_std`, Binary, and Library are named and are not claimed shipped." },
  { number := 551, text := "63. A system call boundary is named and is not a proved theorem. Wild Child is the typed linker in Clause 3.49 and is not claimed implemented. Slake remains the compiler in Clause 3.2. The Slake row of the stack table is not a second repository. Fargo stays unnumbered. Fargo elevation is the gates in Clause 3.47 and is not claimed implemented. The words Process, Parallelism, and Concurrency are named here and are not synonyms." },
  { number := 552, text := "64. The identifiers `MessagePass`, `SWMR`, `MWMR`, `HwAtomic`, and `Ordering` are named here. `SWMR` is one writer and many readers. `MWMR` is many writers and many readers. Those two names are structure modes. A data race on a cell that is not atomic is Reject. A C11 data race and a C++ data race are Reject before emit or Fault under Step (Clause 3.53)." },
  { number := 553, text := "65. The identifiers `Lock`, `Mutex`, `OnceLock`, and `Futex` are named here. `Once` initializes a block exactly once (Clause 3.35). `OnceLock` is that same once cell with a payload. The first writer receives `LoanMut`. Later readers receive `LoanShared`. A second writer is Reject. This document MUST record that every lock name is named. Linux `READ_ONCE`, `WRITE_ONCE`, and `smp` helpers are named in the catalog and are not a present theorem." },
  { number := 554, text := "66. Explicit packed vector operations remain the SIMD topic. Automatic packed vector operations are not a SIMD proof. Treating automatic packed vector operations as a SIMD proof is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. `HazardPointers`, an `Epoch`, `QSBR`, and `RCU` are the reclamation protocols in item 33. `ABA` means a node address is reused after free. Tagged pointers and generation counters are the named tools for that failure. Those identifiers are named here and are not present theories. Proofs treat `FMA` single rounding as the reference, not an `AVX2` multiply followed by an add (item 14). Those proofs are not claimed done. `BMI2` scalar results and `AVX2` vector code MAY share one compute kernel only when the scalar result is inserted into a named lane by an explicit instruction, and the vector memory operand keeps the documented 32 byte alignment from item 14. A compute kernel here is one routine that performs the operation. It is not the operating system kernel. This document does not invent a further calling convention. When a feature bit is clear, the program MUST detect that clear bit during execution and MUST use the scalar reference. This document does not claim that a particular feature dispatcher is implemented. The symbolic Supercompiler, which is the Smart Symbolic Supercompiler specified in `0004_SSSSS.md`, works by delta reconciliation in which a neural path assists. The Supercompiler is evidence based. It adapts to the machine it runs on. It does not keep an assumption that has not first been tested by experiment. It improves itself scientifically. The order is fixed: build a model, then test every model against evidence in a systematic and methodical way, then improve itself. That fixed order is how the Supercompiler improves a model. It is not a replacement for the intended order of delta reconciliation stated in this item. The same input is run through the candidate variants. Those variants are the explicit `AVX2`, `FMA`, and `BMI2` forms, the scalar reference, and alternative forms of the same operation. The outputs are compared and richly turned back into source. The intended order is comparison, a rich reading of compiled code back as source, weighting, ranking, and a Lean proof. The neural reconciliation step MAY weight and rank them toward security, toward performance, or toward both, as `0004_SSSSS.md` Clause 4.8 already allows. This item does not name the tool that turns machine code back into source and does not specify the weight formula. There is no separate numeric weight formula. The Supercompiler ranks candidate rewrites locally, using the known hardware specification of the machine it is running on. The tool that turns machine code back into source stays unnamed. Every observed delta becomes a proven rule, a rejected rule with its counterexample, or an open scenario. A proven rule is a delta that Lean has proved keeps the rules consistent, which is the acceptance rule in `0004_SSSSS.md` Clause 4.8. A rejected rule keeps its counterexample and is not accepted. An open scenario is not accepted. The neural path does not replace that Lean proof. Proven rules feed back into the Supercompiler rule set. Proven rules MAY be contributed to a peer to peer gossip network. A contributed rule is timestamped against the Bitcoin `timechain`. The timestamp method is similar to `OpenTimeStamps` and is not `OpenTimeStamps`. The same rules MAY be contributed to a peer to peer git network of the `Radicle` kind. This document does not say the network is `Radicle`, and it does not claim a gossip client or a git client is implemented. The exact timestamp format and the exact gossip protocol stay open items in the residual clause. Call this the symbolic bitter lesson, which means rules are generated and checked by machinery rather than written by hand. That generation and that check are not claimed done. An observed speedup, or matching output on sample inputs, is evidence. It is not proof. Equivalence MUST be shown in Systems Lean, and `FMA` rounding MUST be handled explicitly. A sample speedup, or matching output on sample inputs, remains evidence. Equivalence of a rewrite is still a Systems Lean proof, and `FMA` single rounding stays explicit in that proof. An experiment decides which model is kept, rejected, or left open. The experiment does not replace the Systems Lean equivalence proof. The reference side of delta reconciliation remains the major compilers named in Clause 6.3. Comparing these candidate variants does not replace that set. That classification records the delta. It does not drop an open emit or behavior delta (item 27). This document names `src/systems/SystemsLean/SimdTargetFeatures.lean` as the checker that encodes the three features `AVX2`, `FMA`, and `BMI2`. Its guards MUST assert the three feature names, the three `CPUID` bits, the three tokens, the 32 byte aligned `AVX2` requirement, `FMA` single rounding as the reference, and that `BMI2` is scalar. This document does not claim that checker file is present. The checker stays open until that file exists and the guards are proved. These sentences do not make an automatic packed vector operation into a SIMD proof. Treating an automatic packed vector operation as a SIMD proof remains Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. These sentences do not make CompCert verified compilation done, do not make the bootstrapped Slake typecheck done, and do not make byte identity of emit true (Clause 6.3). These sentences do not make the full vector instruction set into a completion condition (item 22). A full opcode catalog is not claimed done (item 14)." },
  { number := 555, text := "\tThe supercompiler is evidence based and adapts to the machine it runs on." },
  { number := 556, text := "\tAn assumption is not kept before an experiment." },
  { number := 557, text := "\tThe order is build a model, test every model against evidence, then improve." },
  { number := 558, text := "\tRanking is local from the known hardware specification of that machine." },
  { number := 559, text := "\tThere is no separate numeric weight formula." },
  { number := 560, text := "\tA sample speedup remains evidence and is not the Systems Lean equivalence proof." },
  { number := 561, text := "\tFMA single rounding stays the reference." },
  { number := 562, text := "\tProved rules may be contributed to a peer to peer gossip network, timestamped on the Bitcoin timechain in a way similar to OpenTimeStamps and not OpenTimeStamps, and to a peer to peer git network of the Radicle kind that is not claimed to be Radicle." },
  { number := 563, text := "\tNo client is claimed running." },
  { number := 564, text := "\tThe exact timestamp format and the exact gossip protocol stay open." },
  { number := 565, text := "\tThe decompiler stays unnamed." },
  { number := 566, text := "\tThe opcode catalog stays not claimed done." },
  { number := 567, text := "67. The identifiers `AlignedBuf`, `ValidView`, `Access`, and `RelPtr` are named here. `CheckBytes` means the bytes match the schema before a view is used. `ValidateArchive` means the archive tag and checksum match before `RelPtr` is followed. Those two checks are correspondence and are not a present theorem. `AlignedBuf` is a buffer whose base meets the stated alignment. A `ZeroView` records whether the layout is packed or C representation. Packed layout is explicit. C representation stays first (item 72). `CheckBytes` and `ValidateArchive` are not a `serde` library." },
  { number := 568, text := "68. `FixOS` remains the vision in Clause 4.4 and is not claimed shipped. v28 describes `FixOS` as a compiled store shaped like Nix that uses `BLAKE3` and `Bao`, and it relies on hashes, durability, and Wild Child. The product name fit has no specification number. v28 describes fit as history shaped like `Git` that uses `BLAKE3` and `Bao`, and it relies on objects like `FixOS`. A file system on beastdb relies on beastdb, a file descriptor, and durability, and it has no specification number. Store shape for those names is not claimed implemented. The tree named `bao-tree` is not `FixOS` and is not fit. v28 describes Majestic Memex as lookup and graph storage that relies on beastdb or on a file system on beastdb. Specification 0012 owns that product. This document does not restate that product as a theorem." },
  { number := 569, text := "69. An extra owner column is later work. Two reads are not a counted owner of two. An interior field is a range loan. The default `malloc` path has no refs field. Later `allocator` names share the interface in item 18 and are not claimed implemented." },
  { number := 570, text := "70. Gates A through F are named here. A crate that has completed only Gate A is not Safe. Treating that crate as Safe is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Proofs of gates D through F MAY lag. Copy on write that Fargo lowers becomes `BorrowOrOwned` unless uniqueness tests exist. fargo stays unnumbered." },
  { number := 571, text := "71. The identifiers `Corresponds`, `toSafeC`, and `toRust` are named here. Rust `Box` maps to `UniqueBlock`. `Rc` and `Arc` map to a shared owner that already has a header (Clause 3.27). `Cow` maps to `BorrowOrOwned`. `Vec` is a `UniqueBlock` buffer whose length and capacity are Bits. `Pin` means the address must not move (Clause 3.54). `Send` and `Sync` say which values may move or be loaned across threads, and they are not finished models (item 9). Those maps are correspondence and are not a finished isomorphism. `ObjectCow`, `PageCow`, and `BorrowOrOwned` stay distinct." },
  { number := 572, text := "72. `Cell` and `RefCell` are interior mutability and are not `AtomicCell`. `UnsafeCell` is the interior cell those wrap, and a read of those bytes is not an atomic operation. `Cell`, `RefCell`, and `UnsafeCell` can stash a pointer. Rust that Fargo cannot encode stays outside the encoded fragment for that crate. An ABI alignment is the alignment in the data layout string (item 22) and is not a present theorem. This document MUST name C representation padding first as the padding rule. This document MUST name not all of `rustc` as the `rustc` bound." },
  { number := 573, text := "73. This document MUST name the Phase 0 pair. The Phase 0 pair is a linear `LockToken` and `AffineUnlock`. Phase 0 is not done in this specifications repository. A Phase 0 milestone is not a completion theorem. The v28 words linear ticket in that phase 0 line name this `LockToken`. They do not name a second ticket type. A barrier arrival ticket stays the ticket in Clause 3.35." },
  { number := 574, text := "74. Drop of a lock guard is `AffineUnlock`, and that drop releases the lock (Clause 3.35). `spin_lock` pairs with `spin_unlock`. An interrupt safe lock does not sleep. A sleep capable lock may sleep. Spin lock and condition variable are named here. `SeqLock` is named and its proof may lag. `LockOrder` comes later and is not claimed. `SWMR` and `MWMR` are the structure modes in item 64." },
  { number := 575, text := "75. Populate, locked, huge, and fixed map flags are named. A huge flag is not a second `PageCow` copy (item 36). Later map work is not claimed done. Product clean emit has no hidden heap. Interrupts and an interrupt model are not claimed proved. Fork page copy on write on a private mapping is `PageCow` at 4096 bytes (Clause 3.40)." },
  { number := 576, text := "76. Read copy update is not the page cache. The page cache is counted pages, mappings, and a dirty or locked state, and it is not read copy update. Treating the page cache as `RCU` is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. `glibc` allocation is not a proved `UniqueBlock`. Systems Lean and Slake remain not ready for use." },
  { number := 577, text := "77. The pass fixtures include a field loan that ends before free, a sequentially consistent fetch add, and an arena that ends its children (item 87). The `typer` may accept them. They are not claimed green in this specifications repository. The pass fixtures also include a `CopyBits` loop on a natural number, `MoveOwner` to a `callee` that frees, a `Mutex` lock and unlock, two `RwLock` readers then one writer after they unlock, an `ObjectCow` share and then a copying write, and `malloc` typed as `UniqueBlock` on the first `LibcSL` surface. A proof hole is not success. Non-goals are bans. There is no early isomorphism theorem." },
  { number := 578, text := "78. The two Safes remain `SafeKernel` and `SafeCompCert`. The `typer` matches Step as Clause 3.21 defines Step. `iris-lean` is not required and is not a dependency. Until Wild Child exists, linking stays in the trusted computing base (Clause 3.49). LLVM emit is not a CompCert proved path." },
  { number := 579, text := "79. `IOMMU` and `DMA` are named map modes and are not claimed proved (item 12). A capability is a file descriptor or a `LockToken`, and a capability is not Bits. `CHERI` and `MTE` stay optional hardware tags. Hardware tagging does not replace `UniqueBlock`. Treating hardware tags as a `UniqueBlock` replacement is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Other holes need other types." },
  { number := 580, text := "80. The product `grok-oss` is not the `SafeC` package. Treating `grok-oss` as `SafeC` is Cons False in the Systems Lean proofs module. Systems Lean and Slake remain not ready for use." },
  { number := 581, text := "81. Cons is a layer on the definitions of `SafeMem`, `SafeThr`, `SafeLayout`, `PanicFree`, `LockFree`, and `AtomicTxn`, on every Fargo crate row for Gate B and Gate C, on every `LinkFace` export, and on every emit path. That emit layer distinguishes `CompCertFragment` from `KernelModelOk`. Those layers are not claimed proved. `UniqueBlock` together with `CopyHandle` of that owner, when no alias protocol allows it, is the vulnerability class of use after free after a stash the type allowed, and that clash is Cons False. A clash fails the build when Cons is wired. Heap omega as an owner is a refusal. Cons is not completeness. Cons together with `UbClass` does not imply constant time." },
  { number := 582, text := "82. Undefined behavior class maps are `fromC` and `fromLang`. Use after free, invalid free, a sequence point violation, following a null pointer, and pointer provenance are Reject before emit or Fault under Step (Clause 3.53). The product table is not claimed complete." },
  { number := 583, text := "83. Iterator invalidation, this after lifetime, placement new lifetime, and `destructor` during unwind are Reject before emit or Fault under Step (Clause 3.53). The `destructor` during unwind class does not apply when that behavior is specified. The product table is not claimed complete." },
  { number := 584, text := "84. A proof sticker is not success. Reproduce by rules names the operator loop. A delta is first class. Reproduce step 1 takes a real artifact, and that artifact is C, C++, a Rust crate, an ELF, a system call slice, or a beastdb leaf. That step is not claimed done. Reproduce step 3 runs Cons on those rules, and if the rules contradict one another the characterization is wrong. A rewrite that ignores `SafeCt`, `UbClass`, `UniqueBlock`, and loans is not the rewrite this plan is for. A rewrite bar is not a specification number. Not constant time is not a `SafeCt` on switch. Cons together with `UbClass` does not imply constant time (item 81). The destination of `sleanify` is a Systems Lean agent skill, discovered the same way as project skills, and that skill is not shipped. The north star means rewrite and prove the software that actually runs. That north star is not a phase 0 done condition. Lean Machine is specification 0013, it is ongoing, and it is not ready for use. `sleanify` is one skill shaped input: a neural path proposes a characterization, and the symbolic path is Cons, `SafeC`, and `UbClass`." },
  { number := 585, text := "85. Legal Lean and Holy Lean remain named components of specification 0013 without separate numbers. Companion encodings MUST treat glossary words as `inductive` types. Glossary words MUST NOT clash as if they were one constructor. A glossary word is added in Clause 3 before it is used in a later clause." },
  { number := 586, text := "86. The v28 Grok Build prompt instructions, the `cache-bust` marker, and the END PROMPT line are prompt process. They MUST NOT be read as 0002 language law." },
  { number := 587, text := "87. Pass fixtures named in the v28 catalog, including a `CopyBits` natural number loop, `MoveOwner` to a freeing `callee`, a field loan that ends before free, `Mutex` lock and unlock increment, two `RwLock` readers then one writer, `ObjectCow` share then copy, sequentially consistent fetch add, an arena that ends two children, and typed `malloc` as `UniqueBlock`, are not claimed green in this specifications repository." },
  { number := 588, text := "88. The Linux kernel memory model, the C memory model, and the C++ memory model are required (item 17). `IOMMU` and `DMA` are named and are not claimed proved. When the memory layout of updated system code and of programs in flight stays consistent, a restart is not required, and a running kernel is not claimed verified. A write ahead log attached to beastdb stays owned by specification 0003. The `typer` matches Step and `iris-lean` is not required (item 29). A larger hardware page for `PageCow` is allowed for that one client and is not a second copy (item 36). When a profile turns `SafeCt` on, timing, cache behavior, and page faults do not carry Secret (item 31). `RwLock` fairness is not claimed. An interrupt model is not claimed proved (v28 section 20)." },
  { number := 589, text := "89. The v28 lake package home is leftover in the Systems Lean product tree and is not a public product repository of its own. A canonical public GitHub organization for systems-lean and beastdb is out of scope for this one client system. That is a decision (item 37). Multiplicity omega on Bits is not heap omega as an owner. CompCert progress meters that say proved MUST NOT be copied into a `SafeC` README as a theorem. Incremental linking is a later Wild goal and is not a `SafeC` phase (v28 section 22.3)." },
  { number := 590, text := "90. Holy Lean, `SafeC`, and Lean Machine MUST NOT be mashed into one module. `/sleanify` has no specification number and is not a shipped skill." },
  { number := 591, text := "91. The exact timestamp format stays open." },
  { number := 592, text := "92. The exact gossip protocol stays open." },
  { number := 593, text := "" },
  { number := 594, text := "## 7. Changelog" },
  { number := 595, text := "" },
  { number := 596, text := "### 0.1.0 - 20260831" },
  { number := 597, text := "" },
  { number := 598, text := "Current version line is 0.1.0 by operator order. Changelog history records that a 1.0.0 line existed. Application and neurosymbolic integration for SSSSS live in `0004_SSSSS.md`. Lean Machine is specified in `0013_Lean_Machine.md`. This same 0.1.0 line records that the reference side of delta reconciliation is the major compilers, that CompCert `ccomp`, `CompCertTSO`, gcc, LLVM, and `cc` are in that set, and that being a reference target does not satisfy Clause 4.2 item 6. `CompCertTSO`, gcc, LLVM, and `cc` do not satisfy that item. This same 0.1.0 line records that the `FastSafeC` balance rule is the overlap of `SafeC` and `FastC` together with the named metrics in Clause 4.2 item 6, that the proof of that balance has not been carried out, and that the rule is not an open question." },
  { number := 599, text := "" },
  { number := 600, text := "This 0.1.0 entry also records clarifying clauses for Safe judgments. Definitions in Clause 3.11 through Clause 3.57 name `SafeMem`, `SafeThr`, `SafeLayout`, `KernelModelOk`, `SafeKernel`, `CompCertFragment`, `CompCertRefines`, `SafeCompCert`, Reject, Fault, Step, `TyperSound`, `PanicFree`, `PanicFreeAOT`, Bits, `UniqueBlock`, an alias protocol, five passing modes, grades mapped onto multiplicities 0, 1, and omega, four profiles, words that are not synonyms, correspondence before isomorphism, Cons, `container_of`, the lock catalog, `LockToken`, `LockFree`, the four transaction words, `ObjectCow`, `PageCow`, `BorrowOrOwned`, `PosixFork`, Secret, `DontFork`, `WipeOnConsume`, `SafeCt`, Fargo gates A through F, the emit split, `LinkFace`, `ThreadShared`, `ProcessShared`, `AtomicCell`, correspondence maps as statements, `UbClass`, `ZeroView`, `Pinned`, wait free and obstruction free, `UsesLock`, `LockHandled`, and Reject fixtures. Those clauses keep Slake primary emit as freestanding C and keep `HostedLinuxLibc` additional. This same 0.1.0 line records the v28 cache bust fold: glossary words, standing rules, Reject fixtures, Cons fixtures, `UbClass` names, Fargo gates, profiles, and residual honesty that the `SafeC` lake package is leftover in the Systems Lean product tree and is not a shipped product in this specifications repository. The Operator assigned number 0015 to the session bus, and the numbered file is not in this tree because the draft is missing. No numbered `SafeC` file was created." },
  { number := 601, text := "" },
  { number := 602, text := "Requirements in Clause 4.5 and conformance items in Clause 5 keep `SafeCompCert` implying `SafeKernel`, keep the converse false, keep the named Cons fixtures false, keep CompCert `ccomp` on a Safe path only when `SafeCompCert` holds, and keep an LLVM or `cc` path from being called proved by CompCert when only `SafeKernel` holds. CompCert (`ccomp`) is one metric. It is optional. It is not the only metric. An unmarked freestanding C emit is not required to be compiled with `ccomp`. When `SafeC` is not combined with `FastC`, inclusion of `ccomp` is optional. `FastSafeC` is the overlap of `SafeC` and `FastC`, and for `FastSafeC`, `ccomp` is also optional. A freestanding C emit marked `FastC` that is not combined with `SafeC` is performance priority and does not require CompCert (`ccomp`). A `FastC` mark does not make byte identity of emit true, and a `FastSafeC` mark does not waive that match. Heap use of omega as an owner is a refusal, and that refusal is not the many uses of Bits. fargo stays unnumbered. Proved ACID for beastdb and tries that do not use locks stay in `0003_beastdb.md`. Clause 6.4 items 41 through 86 name sampled v28 topics that were silent, including banned theorem names `Perfect` and `ProvablyPerfect`, `AtomicCell`, `AtomicTxn`, `UsesLock`, `LockHandled`, `LinkFace`, beastdb `Props`, the `memcpy equation`, a `shift exponent out of range`, the `sleanify observation list`, that `Slake is not its own repository`, that `Rust atomic types map to AtomicCell`, the `default trait object fat pointer fields`, `CAS on UniqueBlock pointer Reject`, and `CAS on non-AtomicCell Reject`. Items 55 through 86 name the remaining silent coverage topics, including `framing, locks, FDs, and UAF`, `residual means no model yet`, `C representation padding first`, `not all of rustc`, `Linux once and smp helpers`, `every lock name is named`, the `Phase 0 pair`, `glossary as inductives`, and `glossary words do not clash`. This same 0.1.0 line records `SafeC` depth: the `ref/` pin purpose, freestanding ISO C as ISO/IEC 9899:2024 with the pin file absent because the license forbids it, the memory models, live update without a required restart when layouts stay consistent, one client, the `PageCow` copy of one 4096 byte page, `SafeCt` channels, the `typer` matching Step, and a canonical public GitHub organization out of scope. The same line states the remaining v28 rules in the clauses that own them, including locks, maps, Cons as a layer, undefined behavior classes, and reproduce by rules. Version remains 0.1.0. These sentences do not publish a new SemVer. This same 0.1.0 line records that the typecheck MUST cover all Slake code, including theorems, that `FastC` is not an exception, that security priority is not an exception, that developers and agents MUST always run that typecheck, and that a user who is building finished code MAY skip running that step without exempting the Slake code and without making the bootstrap typecheck done. Byte identity stays required, is not claimed true, and a `FastC` mark does not waive it. This same 0.1.0 line records that Clause 6.4 item 14 and item 66 define the target features `AVX2`, `FMA` (`FMA3`), and `BMI2`, name `src/systems/SystemsLean/SimdTargetFeatures.lean` as an open checker, and do not claim that checker is proved, do not claim a feature dispatcher is implemented, do not claim a full opcode catalog is done, do not make CompCert verified compilation done, do not make the Slake bootstrap typecheck done, and do not make byte identity of emit true. This same 0.1.0 line records that the Supercompiler in Clause 6.4 item 66 is evidence based, that it adapts to the machine it runs on, that it does not keep an assumption that has not first been tested by experiment, that it improves itself scientifically, and that the fixed order is to build a model, then test every model against evidence in a systematic and methodical way, then improve itself. That fixed order is how the Supercompiler improves a model. It is not a replacement for the intended order of delta reconciliation in item 66. The Supercompiler ranks candidate rewrites locally, using the known hardware specification of the machine it is running on. There is no separate numeric weight formula. The decompiler tool stays unnamed. A sample speedup, or matching output on sample inputs, remains evidence. Equivalence of a rewrite is still a Systems Lean proof, and `FMA` single rounding stays explicit in that proof. An experiment decides which model is kept, rejected, or left open. The experiment does not replace the Systems Lean equivalence proof. Proven rules MAY be contributed to a peer to peer gossip network. A contributed rule is timestamped against the Bitcoin `timechain`. The timestamp method is similar to `OpenTimeStamps` and is not `OpenTimeStamps`. The same rules MAY be contributed to a peer to peer git network of the `Radicle` kind. This document does not say the network is `Radicle`, and it does not claim a gossip client or a git client is implemented. The exact timestamp format and the exact gossip protocol stay open. These sentences do not invent a numeric weight formula, do not name a decompiler tool, do not claim a gossip client is implemented, do not claim a git client is implemented, do not say the network is `Radicle`, do not claim `OpenTimeStamps` itself, do not claim the network already runs, and do not claim a full opcode catalog is done. Version remains 0.1.0." },
